<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Large Reasoning Models (LRMs) often exhibit structural fragility in complex reasoning tasks, failing to produce correct answers even after successfully deriving valid intermediate steps.
Through systematic analysis, we observe that these failures frequently stem not from a lack of reasoning capacity, but from a deficiency in self-regulatory control, where valid logic is destabilized by uncontrolled exploration or the failure to recognize logical sufficiency.
Motivated by this observation, we propose Metacognitive Behavioral Tuning (MBT), a post-training framework that explicitly injects metacognitive behaviors into the model’s thought process.
MBT implements this via two complementary formulations: (1) MBT-S, which synthesizes rigorous reasoning traces from scratch, and (2) MBT-R, which rewrites the student’s initial traces to stabilize intrinsic exploration patterns.
Experiments across multi-hop QA benchmarks demonstrate that MBT consistently outperforms baselines, achieving notable gains on challenging benchmarks.
By effectively eliminating reasoning collapse, MBT achieves higher accuracy with significantly reduced token consumption, demonstrating that internalizing metacognitive strategies leads to more stable and robust reasoning.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_keywords">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_keywords">Keywords: </h6>Machine Learning, ICML
</div><span id="id1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_affiliationnotice"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">affiliationnotice: </span>Equal contribution</span></span></span>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Large Language Models (LLMs) have recently evolved into Large Reasoning Models (LRMs) by internalizing the chain-of-thought process through large-scale reinforcement learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.
This paradigm shift unlocks test-time scaling, a new dimension of scaling where performance improves as the model allocates more time to thinking <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Snell et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Agarwal et al., 2025a</a>)</cite>. Modern LRMs demonstrate exceptional proficiency in complex tasks, particularly within STEM domains <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jaech et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Team et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.22508v1/real_case.svg" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:429/442;" width="429" height="442"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S1.F1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 1</span>: </span><span id="S1.F1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">A representative reasoning trace generated by Qwen3-4B. Despite correctly deriving the answer in intermediate steps, the model ultimately discards it due to an unverified self-imposed constraint. This illustrates a critical deficiency in metacognitive monitoring, where valid logic is overridden by uncontrolled exploration rather than a lack of reasoning capacity.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S1.F2" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.22508v1/Figure1_wo_text.svg" id="S1.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/169;" width="476" height="169"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S1.F2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 2</span>: </span><span id="S1.F2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Prevalence of Answer-Inclusive Errors across MHQA benchmarks using Qwen3-8B. Incorrect predictions are stratified into Answer-Inclusive (derived but discarded) and Answer-Exclusive (never derived). The substantial proportion of Answer-Inclusive errors reveals that performance gaps frequently stem from a failure to monitor and preserve correct intermediate conclusions, rather than insufficient knowledge or retrieval capacity.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As reasoning becomes a central component of model behavior, the concept of hallucination requires re-evaluation.
Traditionally, hallucination refers to the generation of factually incorrect content <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ji et al., 2023</a>)</cite>.
However, with the emergence of LRMs, explicit internal chains of thought make the underlying logic observable, enabling a process-centric analysis of model failures.
This suggests extending the concept of hallucination from the outcome level to the process level.
Our analysis reveals that failures often arise not from a lack of reasoning capacity, but from a lack of logical control over the problem-solving process.
When models lose this control, they begin to hallucinate invalid logical steps by inventing non-existent constraints or fabricating justifications, which leads to incorrect conclusions.</p>
</div>
<section id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Our Findings</h5>

<div id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To quantify the prevalence of these process-level deviations, we analyze reasoning traces from Multi-Hop Question Answering (MHQA) benchmarks.
We begin with a representative example in Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Mirroring the Mind: Distilling Human-LikeMetacognitive Strategies into Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, generated by Qwen3-4B, which illustrates a recurring failure pattern.
In this retrieval-augmented scenario asking, <span id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“Who is the child of Caroline LeRoy’s spouse?”</span>, the model successfully retrieves the necessary facts: it identifies the spouse as Daniel Webster and locates his son, Fletcher Webster.
However, despite correctly deriving the answer, the model falls into a self-imposed constraint trap, assuming the child must be from their specific marriage.
The model fails to monitor or verify the validity of this new constraint, leading it to unjustifiably reject the correct candidate.
This example vividly demonstrates that the failure stems not from insufficient capacity, but from a lack of metacognitive monitoring, specifically the failure to critically evaluate its own logical constraints before overriding a valid conclusion.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We demonstrate that this failure pattern occurs at scale across multiple MHQA benchmarks, including MuSiQue <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Trivedi et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, HotpotQA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2018</a>)</cite>, and 2WikiMultiHopQA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ho et al., 2020</a>)</cite>.
We classify reasoning traces to identify instances where the correct answer is successfully derived during intermediate steps yet discarded in the final output.
We term these cases <span id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">answer-inclusive errors</span>.
To distinguish genuine logical derivation from incidental mentions, such as random guesses or rote enumeration, we employ an LLM-as-a-Judge evaluation.
This approach ensures that the presence of the correct answer is explicitly grounded in relevant retrieved evidence.
Figure <a href="#S1.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Mirroring the Mind: Distilling Human-LikeMetacognitive Strategies into Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> reports the prevalence of these verified errors, confirming that a substantial fraction of model failures stem from a critical breakdown in metacognitive monitoring, where the model successfully derives the truth but fails to recognize and preserve it.
Details regarding the specific judge model and evaluation prompts are provided in the Appendix <a href="#A5.SS1" title="E.1 Answer Inclusion Evaluation Prompt ‣ Appendix E Evaluation Prompts ‣ Mirroring the Mind: Distilling Human-LikeMetacognitive Strategies into Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Motivated by these observations, we characterize two dominant failure modes in LRMs: <span id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">overthinking</span> and <span id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">underthinking</span>.
While prior works in mathematical reasoning defined overthinking as generating an excessive number of solution candidates and underthinking as frequent thought switching that hinders deep exploration <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., </a>)</cite>, we refine these definitions to better capture the structural dynamics of reasoning in MHQA.
We define overthinking in correctly answered samples as post-solution stagnation, where the model successfully derives the answer but engages in unproductive redundancy, such as repetitive confirmation or baseless self-doubt that adds no new confidence.
Conversely, we define underthinking as incorrect samples exhibiting pre-solution stagnation, where the reasoning process becomes trapped in a cycle of revisiting previous hypotheses or repeating the same questions, failing to integrate new information or converge on a solution.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Existing post-training paradigms, particularly reinforcement learning methods like RLVR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lambert et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shao et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yu et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2025b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng et al., 2025</a>)</cite> that rely on outcome-based rewards, can inadvertently exacerbate these instability issues.
Given the high prevalence of answer-inclusive errors, assigning rewards solely based on the final output introduces noisy training signals by indiscriminately penalizing the entire trajectory, even when it encompasses valid intermediate steps <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Deng et al., 2025b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Deng et al., 2025a</a>)</cite>.
Furthermore, because the objective is tied exclusively to the final answer, there is little incentive for the model to optimize the intrinsic quality or stability of the reasoning process, leaving it vulnerable to the uncontrolled deviations described above.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">To address these limitations, we propose <span id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">M</span>etacognitive <span id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">B</span>ehavioral <span id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">T</span>uning (<span id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p5.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">MBT</span>), a simple and fully automated post-training framework that explicitly injects structured metacognitive behaviors into the reasoning process.
MBT offers flexible data construction strategies, injecting metacognitive behaviors either by synthesizing reasoning traces from scratch or by rewriting existing ones.
By fine-tuning on reasoning traces regenerated to exhibit these behaviors, MBT proactively suppresses both overthinking and underthinking.
Furthermore, MBT serves as a foundational alignment stage that refines the exploration space for subsequent reinforcement learning.
This structural stabilization enables the model to maintain logical stability even when optimized with outcome-based rewards.
Extensive experiments demonstrate that MBT consistently improves accuracy and robustness, effectively closing the gap between the model’s latent capacity and its realized performance.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Our main contributions are as follows:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We identify a critical bottleneck in LRMs where valid intermediate reasoning is often destabilized by a deficiency in logic regulation, manifesting as structural failures like overthinking and underthinking. This highlights a significant disparity between the model’s latent reasoning capacity and its ability to effectively monitor and control the progression of its thought process.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose <span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Metacognitive Behavioral Tuning (MBT)</span>, a post-training framework that explicitly injects metacognitive behaviors to stabilize reasoning trajectories. By teaching the model to actively plan, monitor, and verify its logic, MBT refines the exploration space, effectively preventing reasoning collapse and ensuring robust learning during subsequent stages.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We introduce two complementary formulations for behavior injection: synthesizing new traces (MBT-S) and rewriting native traces (MBT-R). Our experiments validate that both strategies effectively induce robust metacognitive control, achieving a superior trade-off between accuracy and efficiency across diverse MHQA benchmarks.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Works</h2>

<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Process Supervision</h5>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Recent research emphasizes supervising intermediate reasoning steps rather than relying solely on sparse outcome rewards.
Process Reward Models (PRMs) have demonstrated that verifying intermediate steps significantly enhances performance in complex reasoning tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lightman et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Uesato et al., 2022</a>)</cite>.
However, these methods typically depend on training external verifiers or collecting expensive step-level annotations.
In contrast, MBT internalizes this supervision directly into the generation policy, enabling the model to inherently monitor and regulate its trajectory without the computational overhead of external test-time verification.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Cognitive Structures</h5>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Integrating cognitive theories into LLMs aims to induce deliberate reasoning.
Approaches like System 2 Attention <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Weston and Sukhbaatar, 2023</a>)</cite> and Plan-and-Solve <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2023</a>)</cite> decouple planning from execution, while Self-Refine <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Madaan et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, Reflexion <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shinn et al., 2023</a>)</cite> and Chain-of-Verification <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dhuliawala et al., 2024</a>)</cite> introduce iterative self-correction.
Recent works extend this by modeling reasoning as an internal search <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xiang et al., 2025</a>)</cite> or identifying behavioral seeds for RL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gandhi et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.
However, these methods often focus on discovery or initial seeding rather than stabilizing the entire trajectory, leaving models prone to structural fragility.
In contrast, MBT explicitly targets the post-training regulation of reasoning to ensure stability and efficiency.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Reasoning Distillation</h5>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A growing body of work focuses on distilling reasoning rationales rather than just final answers.
Methods such as Distilling Step-by-Step <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hsieh et al., 2023</a>)</cite> and Orca <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mukherjee et al., 2023</a>)</cite> show that learning from rich explanation traces allows smaller models to outperform larger baselines.
Similarly, STaR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zelikman et al., 2022</a>)</cite> utilizes self-generated rationales to bootstrap reasoning capabilities.
MBT extends this paradigm by distilling not merely the content of reasoning, but the metacognitive structure itself, thereby transferring the ability to regulate the thought process rather than just the logical steps.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Efficient Reasoning</h5>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Prior works also address reasoning efficiency.
Inference-time methods like TokenSkip <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xia et al., 2025</a>)</cite> compress traces by skipping low-information tokens, while data-centric approaches like LIMOPro <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xiao et al., 2025</a>)</cite> prune functional steps deemed redundant.
Additionally, recent reinforcement learning strategies such as DLER <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2025a</a>)</cite> and ShorterBetter <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yi et al., 2025</a>)</cite> explicitly optimize reasoning efficiency by incorporating length penalties into the reward structure.
While effective for latency, our experiments suggest that aggressive pruning can compromise stability in complex multi-hop scenarios.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2602.22508v1/method.png" id="S2.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:429/220;" width="429" height="220" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S2.F3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 3</span>: </span><span id="S2.F3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">The overall framework of Metacognitive Behavioral Tuning (MBT). We employ two complementary strategies for behavior injection: MBT-S synthesizes rigorous traces from scratch, while MBT-R rewrites the student’s initial traces to stabilize intrinsic exploration. The model then internalizes these behaviors via Supervised Fine-Tuning (SFT), followed by Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO) to enhance reasoning robustness.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Method</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we introduce <span id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">M</span>etacognitive <span id="S3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">B</span>ehavioral <span id="S3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">T</span>uning (MBT), a streamlined post-training framework designed to enhance reasoning stability and efficiency by explicitly injecting metacognitive behaviors into LRMs.
The core objective is to refine how models plan, monitor, and stabilize their reasoning trajectories, preventing the structural failures of overthinking and underthinking.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To accommodate different training needs, MBT provides two complementary data construction strategies, distinguished by their dependency on the student model’s initial reasoning traces.
<span id="S3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(1) MBT-S (Synthesis)</span> constructs reasoning paths from scratch using a teacher model.
This formulation enforces a rigorous metacognitive structure independent of the student’s initial capabilities, effectively serving as a cold start for reasoning behaviors.
<span id="S3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(2) MBT-R (Rewriting)</span> refines the initial reasoning traces generated by the student model.
By identifying and rewriting unstable segments while preserving valid intermediate deductions, this approach aligns the student’s existing knowledge with structured metacognitive control.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The overall MBT pipeline consists of three stages: (1) Acquisition of initial reasoning samples from the student model (for MBT-R), (2) Injection of metacognitive behaviors via teacher-guided synthesis or rewriting, and (3) Internalization through supervised fine-tuning (SFT) and subsequent reinforcement learning.
An overview of the pipeline is illustrated in Figure <a href="#S2.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ Efficient Reasoning ‣ 2 Related Works ‣ Mirroring the Mind: Distilling Human-LikeMetacognitive Strategies into Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Initial Reasoning Trace Generation</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let</p>
<table id="S3.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}=\{(q_{i},c_{i},a_{i}^{*})\}_{i=1}^{N}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}=\{(q_{i},c_{i},a_{i}^{*})\}_{i=1}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">denote a MHQA dataset, where <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is a query, <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the context (either retrieved or given), and <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is the gold answer.
Given an input <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(q,c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(q,c)</annotation></semantics></math>, a student reasoning model <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\text{S}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mtext>S</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\text{S}}</annotation></semantics></math> generates a reasoning trace <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> and a predicted answer <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{a}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(p,\hat{a})\leftarrow M_{\text{S}}(q,c)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mtext>S</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(p,\hat{a})\leftarrow M_{\text{S}}(q,c).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Metacognitive Reasoning Trace Construction</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now describe how metacognitively grounded reasoning traces are constructed.
Central to both approaches is the injection of a structured reasoning flow comprising five distinct metacognitive phases:
<span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(1) Understanding and Filtering</span>, where the model explicitly re-states the core goal and filters out irrelevant information to ensure a robust grasp of requirements;
<span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(2) Planning</span>, where it outlines a high-level strategy and breaks the problem into manageable sub-tasks before deduction;
<span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(3) Execution and Monitoring</span>, where the model executes steps while actively questioning the logical soundness of each transition;
<span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(4) Self-Correction</span>, where it simulates doubt upon detecting deviations, identifies logical gaps, and adjusts the path; and
<span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(5) Verification</span>, where the model rigorously examines the conclusion against edge cases and alternative scenarios to determine whether further exploration is required, ultimately establishing high confidence before deciding to terminate the process.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.2.1 </span>MBT-S: Metacognitive Trace Synthesis</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the synthesis-based formulation, MBT-S constructs reasoning traces from scratch using a teacher model <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\text{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mtext>T</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\text{T}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Given a query <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, context <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, and gold answer <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\text{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mtext>T</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\text{T}}</annotation></semantics></math> generates a fully structured reasoning trace:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{i}^{\text{meta}}\sim M_{\text{T}}(q_{i},c_{i},a_{i}^{*})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi><mtext>meta</mtext></msubsup><mo>∼</mo><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mtext>T</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{i}^{\text{meta}}\sim M_{\text{T}}(q_{i},c_{i},a_{i}^{*}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">MBT-S does not rely on the student’s initial reasoning traces.
Instead, it produces idealized trajectories that strictly adhere to the five-phase structure.
This formulation is particularly advantageous for establishing a rigorous metacognitive baseline, serving as a cold start mechanism that enforces stability independent of the student model’s inherent biases.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.2.2 </span>MBT-R: Metacognitive Trace Rewriting</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the rewriting-based formulation, MBT-R injects metacognitive behaviors by restructuring the initial reasoning traces generated by the student model.
Given an initial trace <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> produced by <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\textrm{S}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mtext>S</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\textrm{S}}</annotation></semantics></math>, the teacher model rewrites the trace conditioned on the query, context, and gold answer:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{i}^{\text{meta}}\sim M_{\text{T}}(q_{i},c_{i},p_{i},a_{i}^{*})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi><mtext>meta</mtext></msubsup><mo>∼</mo><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mtext>T</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{i}^{\text{meta}}\sim M_{\text{T}}(q_{i},c_{i},p_{i},a_{i}^{*}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Unlike MBT-S, this approach explicitly utilizes the initial reasoning trace <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> to preserve the student’s intrinsic exploration patterns while imposing the structured flow of planning, monitoring, and verification.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To ensure precise and context-aware guiding, we employ a multi-turn prompting strategy.
In the first turn, the teacher model is provided with the query <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, context <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, and gold answer <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a^{*}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{*}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, and is instructed to generate a correct solution to internalize the optimal reasoning logic.
In the subsequent turn, the teacher rewrites the student’s initial trace <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> based on this established understanding.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">This rewriting process addresses both correct and incorrect initial traces.
When the initial trace <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> leads to an incorrect answer, the teacher does not simply discard the text.
Instead, it simulates a metacognitive self-correction process, where the reasoning path explicitly identifies the flaw via a monitoring step, rejects the erroneous logic, and naturally redirects the trajectory toward the correct solution <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a^{*}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{*}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.
This design teaches the model not only how to structure valid thoughts but also how to detect and recover from its own reasoning errors, thereby effectively regulating its intrinsic thought process.
The detailed prompt templates for both synthesis and rewriting strategies are available in the Appendix <a href="#A5.SS2" title="E.2 Metacognitively Grounded Reasoning Trace Prompt ‣ Appendix E Evaluation Prompts ‣ Mirroring the Mind: Distilling Human-LikeMetacognitive Strategies into Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Post-training with Metacognitive Samples</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The metacognitive traces constructed in the previous stage are used to further train the student model <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\text{S}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mtext>S</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\text{S}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
MBT adopts a two-stage post-training procedure consisting of Supervised Fine-Tuning (SFT) to internalize the behavioral structure, followed by Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO) to enhance generalization and robustness.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.3.1 </span>Supervised Fine-Tuning</h4>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We first perform supervised fine-tuning on the constructed metacognitive traces to inject the five-phase reasoning structure.
Let <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the policy of the student model parameterized by <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>.
The SFT objective is to minimize the negative log-likelihood of the metacognitive traces <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{\text{meta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>p</mi><mtext>meta</mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{\text{meta}}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{SFT}}(\theta)=-\mathbb{E}_{(q,p^{\text{meta}})}\left[\sum_{t}\log\pi_{\theta}(y_{t}\mid q,y_{&lt;t})\right]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>SFT</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mtext>meta</mtext></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>t</mi></munder><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{SFT}}(\theta)=-\mathbb{E}_{(q,p^{\text{meta}})}\left[\sum_{t}\log\pi_{\theta}(y_{t}\mid q,y_{&lt;t})\right],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y=(y_{1},\dots,y_{T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=(y_{1},\dots,y_{T})</annotation></semantics></math> represents the sequence of tokens constituting the metacognitive reasoning trace <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{\text{meta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>p</mi><mtext>meta</mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{\text{meta}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
By aligning the student model with these regulated trajectories, SFT effectively refines the exploration space, ensuring that the model’s initial policy for the subsequent reinforcement learning stage is already grounded in stable and logical reasoning patterns.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.3.2 </span>Group Relative Policy Optimization</h4>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">After SFT, we further refine the model using Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shao et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.
We employ GRPO as our reinforcement learning algorithm due to its stability and efficiency in optimizing reasoning models without the need for a separate value function (critic).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Specifically, for each query <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>, GRPO samples a group of <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> outputs <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{o_{1},...,o_{G}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{o_{1},...,o_{G}\}</annotation></semantics></math> from the current policy and optimizes the model using the relative advantages of these outputs within the group.
In our framework, we employ an outcome-based reward formulated as the F1 score between the predicted answer and the gold answer <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>.
This metric provides a soft training signal that encourages text overlap with the ground truth, with finer granularity than binary correctness.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Standard outcome-based RL often suffers from instability when the exploration space is vast and noisy.
However, since the preceding SFT stage has already refined the exploration space with structured metacognitive traces, GRPO can effectively leverage these F1-based outcome signals to reinforce the most efficient and robust reasoning paths.
This allows the model to generalize its metacognitive capabilities to unseen problems while maintaining the structural stability instilled during SFT.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Experiments</h2>

<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Experimental Setup</h3>

<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Datasets</h5>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate MBT under both in-distribution (ID) and out-of-distribution (OOD) settings to distinguish between simple dataset fitting and robust generalization.
Across all experiments, models are trained exclusively on the HotpotQA training set.
To evaluate OOD generalization, we utilize the validation sets of MuSiQue and 2WikiMultiHopQA, which present distinct reasoning structures and evidence compositions.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S4.T1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 1</span>: </span><span id="S4.T1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Reasoning performance of various methods across MHQA benchmarks. Metrics include Exact Match (EM), F1 score, and LLM-as-a-Judge (LLM) accuracy.</span></figcaption>
