</table>
</span></div>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.4 </span>Reasoning Efficiency and Stability</h3>

<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We focus our efficiency and stability analysis on MuSiQue, the most challenging benchmark in our evaluation.
Due to its multi-step complexity and high propensity for reasoning collapse, MuSiQue serves as the most rigorous testbed for assessing a model’s ability to control reasoning length and prevent degeneration under stress.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in Table <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ Performance and Efficiency ‣ 4.2 Evaluation Metrics ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Mirroring the Mind: Distilling Human-LikeMetacognitive Strategies into Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, baseline approaches exhibit significant limitations in balancing efficiency and stability.
Rejection Sampling (RS) tends to amplify redundant reasoning patterns, resulting in bloated average output lengths without proportional accuracy gains.
More critically, efficiency-oriented methods such as TokenSkip and LIMOPro, which rely on artificial pruning or skipping, severely compromise structural stability.
We observe that this heuristic manipulation disrupts reasoning coherence, causing frequent infinite loops (Degeneration Failures).
Paradoxically, this instability inflates the overall inference cost, rendering these methods impractical for complex multi-hop tasks.
Standard GRPO also fails to mitigate this instability; it remains highly vulnerable to reasoning collapse and ineffective at reducing redundancy, particularly at the larger 4B scale.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In contrast, MBT demonstrates superior stability, achieving near-zero degeneration across all model scales.
By internalizing metacognitive regulation rather than applying external constraints, MBT enables the model to recognize logical sufficiency and naturally suppress unnecessary continuation.
MBT thus produces substantially more concise reasoning traces while maintaining high accuracy, resulting in the highest AES among all methods.
These results confirm that MBT offers the most efficient and robust solution for deploying LRMs, effectively eliminating the risk of structural collapse even in the most demanding scenarios.
For further investigations into behavior injection strategies and a detailed qualitative analysis of metacognitive reasoning traces, please refer to the Appendix <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Qualitative Analysis of Metacognitive Reasoning Traces ‣ Mirroring the Mind: Distilling Human-LikeMetacognitive Strategies into Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Discussion</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we analyze the mechanisms driving the effectiveness of MBT and address three critical questions regarding its design and impact: (1) Is the performance gain merely a byproduct of distilling a stronger teacher? (2) Does MBT effectively mitigate the structural failures of overthinking and underthinking? (3) Does the framework successfully inject distinct metacognitive behaviors into the model?
Unless otherwise specified, all analytical experiments in this section are conducted using Qwen3-4B on the challenging MuSiQue benchmark.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Is Metacognitive Behavior Injection Reducible to Reasoning Distillation?</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A natural hypothesis is that the improvements achieved by MBT stem solely from distilling the superior reasoning capabilities of the teacher model, regardless of the metacognitive structure.
To test this, we construct a baseline denoted as gpt-oss-distill.
This baseline follows the identical post-training pipeline (SFT followed by GRPO), but utilizes the raw reasoning traces generated by the teacher model without any explicit metacognitive formatting or behavioral injection.
We apply rejection sampling to retain only correct teacher-generated traces.
This setup allows us to isolate the effect of teacher strength from metacognitive regulation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in Table <a href="#S4.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 4.3 Multi-Hop QA Reasoning Performance ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Mirroring the Mind: Distilling Human-LikeMetacognitive Strategies into Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, gpt-oss-distill achieves high accuracy, in some cases slightly surpassing MBT.
However, this accuracy comes at a significant cost: the model exhibits extreme inefficiency and frequent degeneration, often mimicking the teacher’s verbose exploration without the capacity to terminate effectively.
This results in bloated output lengths and a substantially negative AES, rendering it impractical for real-world deployment.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In contrast, MBT strikes a critical balance.
While maintaining accuracy comparable to the distilled baseline, MBT explicitly injects structural control, enabling the model to reason concisely and stabilize its trajectory.
This results in significantly higher AES and near-zero degeneration.
These findings demonstrate that while raw distillation transfers what to think, MBT is essential for transferring how to regulate that thinking, ensuring that high performance is achieved with the stability and efficiency required for practical application.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F4" class="ltx_figure">
<div id="S5.F4.3" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:293.3pt;height:209.2pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-27.5pt,19.6pt) scale(0.841872086109685,0.841872086109685) ;"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.22508v1/comparing_thinking_ratio_scatter_4B.svg" id="S5.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/343;" width="476" height="343"></object>
</span></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 4</span>: </span><span id="S5.F4.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Structural stability analysis via Overthinking (<math id="S5.F4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{\text{OT}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mtext>OT</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{\text{OT}}</annotation></semantics></math>) vs. Underthinking (<math id="S5.F4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{\text{UT}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mtext>UT</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{\text{UT}}</annotation></semantics></math>) on MuSiQue. While baseline methods exhibit instability by drifting towards premature termination or excessive continuation, MBT-S and MBT-R successfully converge in the stable lower-left region. This confirms that MBT effectively regulates the reasoning process to prevent both extremes.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>Does MBT Mitigate the Structural Failures of Overthinking and Underthinking?</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We next analyze whether MBT effectively addresses the dual failures of reasoning stagnation.
To visualize the effect, we plot the Overthinking Score (<math id="S5.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{\text{OT}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mtext>OT</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{\text{OT}}</annotation></semantics></math>) against the Underthinking Score (<math id="S5.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{\text{UT}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mtext>UT</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{\text{UT}}</annotation></semantics></math>) in Figure <a href="#S5.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 5.1 Is Metacognitive Behavior Injection Reducible to Reasoning Distillation? ‣ 5 Discussion ‣ Mirroring the Mind: Distilling Human-LikeMetacognitive Strategies into Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, where the lower-left corner represents the optimal region of high structural stability.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The plot reveals a distinct separation between MBT-based methods and other baselines.
Models without structured metacognitive supervision tend to scatter towards the extremes, exhibiting either excessive reasoning (high <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{\text{OT}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mtext>OT</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{\text{OT}}</annotation></semantics></math>) or premature termination (high <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{\text{UT}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mtext>UT</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{\text{UT}}</annotation></semantics></math>).
In contrast, both MBT-S and MBT-R cluster in the lower-left region, indicating a simultaneous reduction in both failure modes.
This confirms that MBT does not merely trade one type of error for another, such as reducing overthinking by encouraging premature stopping, but effectively instills a balanced regulatory mechanism that knows both when to explore and when to conclude.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.3 </span>Does MBT Effectively Inject Metacognitive Behaviors?</h3>

<div id="S5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, we examine whether the observed stability stems from the genuine adoption of metacognitive strategies.
Figure <a href="#S5.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 5.3 Does MBT Effectively Inject Metacognitive Behaviors? ‣ 5 Discussion ‣ Mirroring the Mind: Distilling Human-LikeMetacognitive Strategies into Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> compares the Metacognition Scores across various methods.
While baseline models exhibit reasonable reasoning structures, MBT-R achieves the highest score, outperforming all other approaches.
We attribute this distinct advantage to the nature of the rewriting mechanism.
Unlike synthesis from scratch, MBT-R operates on the model’s intrinsic exploration, stabilizing valid but potentially scattered thoughts.
This process of reorganizing and refining its own logic creates a richer display of metacognitive dynamics, as the model must actively demonstrate self-correction and integration to align its original exploration with the imposed structure.
Notably, while the figure highlights results for the 4B model, we observe that the gap in metacognitive scores between MBT and the base model is even more pronounced at smaller scales (e.g., 0.6B, 1.7B). Comprehensive numerical results for all model scales are provided in the Table <a href="#A2.T8" title="Table A8 ‣ Distinct Behavioral Profiles of MBT Variants ‣ B.5 Detailed Analysis of Overthinking, Underthinking, and Metacognitive Behavior ‣ Appendix B Additional Experimental Results ‣ Mirroring the Mind: Distilling Human-LikeMetacognitive Strategies into Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A8</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F5" class="ltx_figure">
<div id="S5.F5.3" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:345.0pt;height:150.5pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-1.7pt,0.7pt) scale(0.990430636471552,0.990430636471552) ;"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.22508v1/compare_MS_bar_v2.svg" id="S5.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/210;" width="476" height="210"></object>
</span></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F5.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 5</span>: </span><span id="S5.F5.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Comparison of Metacognition Scores on the MuSiQue dataset using Qwen3-4B. We evaluate the richness of metacognitive behaviors across various methods. The detailed evaluation prompt and scoring rubric are provided in the Figure <a href="#A5.F9" title="Figure A9 ‣ E.5 Metacognitive Prompting Prompt ‣ Appendix E Evaluation Prompts ‣ Mirroring the Mind: Distilling Human-LikeMetacognitive Strategies into Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A9</span></a>.
</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we identify a critical limitation in LRMs, where reasoning failures frequently stem not from a lack of capacity, but from inadequate monitoring and control over the logical flow.
Our analysis reveals that correct intermediate reasoning is often discarded or destabilized due to the absence of a regulatory mechanism.
To address this, we propose Metacognitive Behavioral Tuning (MBT), a post-training framework offering two complementary formulations (MBT-S and MBT-R) that inject explicit metacognitive behaviors to stabilize reasoning through rigorous monitoring and logical control.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Comprehensive experiments across various scales of models and diverse MHQA benchmarks demonstrate that MBT consistently enhances reasoning accuracy and efficiency.
Notably, the framework exhibits strong generalization to complex MHQA tasks, effectively tackling problems significantly harder than those seen during training.
Behavioral analyses further confirm that MBT mitigates both underthinking and overthinking, indicating that the model has successfully internalized the capacity to monitor and control its own logic, leading to superior structural stability.
Overall, our results suggest that establishing rigorous metacognitive control is as crucial as scaling raw reasoning capacity, highlighting behavioral supervision as a vital avenue for advancing reasoning models.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
<section id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Limitations</h5>

<div id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">MBT’s reliance on a large teacher model introduces additional data preparation costs.
While our results show that these traces generalize effectively across model scales, future work is needed to explore lighter-weight trace generation methods and to extend this framework to domains beyond MHQA.
Broadly, we believe that shifting the focus from what to reason (content accumulation) to how to reason (process regulation) offers a promising direction for the next generation of robust reasoning models.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="Sx1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Impact Statements</h2>

<div id="Sx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This work introduces Metacognitive Behavioral Tuning (MBT), a framework designed to transform Large Reasoning Models (LRMs) from capable but fragile systems into stable and efficient reasoners.
The primary societal impact of this work lies in advancing the reliability and interpretability of AI systems.
By enforcing a structured flow of planning, monitoring, and verification, MBT makes the model’s decision-making process more transparent and auditable, which is crucial for deployment in high-stakes domains such as healthcare and legal analysis where “black-box” reasoning is unacceptable.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Furthermore, MBT contributes to sustainable AI deployment.
By effectively eliminating reasoning collapse and redundant loops, our method significantly reduces inference-time token consumption.
This directly translates to lower computational energy requirements and a reduced carbon footprint for large-scale model serving.
However, we acknowledge that enhanced reasoning and planning capabilities carry dual-use risks, as they could potentially be exploited for malicious purposes, such as sophisticated cyber-attacks or social engineering.
While metacognitive regulation improves logical stability, it does not inherently align moral intent.
Therefore, MBT should be viewed as a tool to enhance capability and stability, which must be rigorously complemented by alignment training and human oversight frameworks to ensure safe application.</p>
</div>
</section>
