<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Answer Inclusion Analysis</h2>

<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Standard evaluation metrics often fail to capture the nuances of the reasoning process of the model.
To address this, we provide a complementary analysis to Figure <a href="#S1.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Mirroring the Mind: Distilling Human-LikeMetacognitive Strategies into Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> by quantifying answer inclusion using two distinct methods: strict string-based matching and a semantic LLM-as-a-Judge evaluation.
Tables <a href="#Ax1.T1" title="Table A1 ‣ Appendix ‣ Mirroring the Mind: Distilling Human-LikeMetacognitive Strategies into Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A1</span></a>–<a href="#Ax1.T3" title="Table A3 ‣ Appendix ‣ Mirroring the Mind: Distilling Human-LikeMetacognitive Strategies into Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A3</span></a> report results across MuSiQue <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Trivedi et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, HotpotQA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2018</a>)</cite>, and 2WikiMultiHopQA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ho et al., 2020</a>)</cite> benchmarks, respectively.
We categorize predictions into correct and incorrect cases based on the final output.
For each reasoning trace, we determine whether the gold answer was identified or derived at any intermediate step regardless of the final outcome.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Substring Match</span> determines whether the gold answer string explicitly appears in the reasoning trace.
While simple and transparent, this method is brittle; it misses paraphrased answers and can yield false positives by matching contextually irrelevant mentions.
To address this limitation, we additionally employ an LLM-as-a-Judge evaluation.
In contrast, the <span id="A1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">LLM_judge</span> follows a more permissive criterion aligned with Figure <a href="#S1.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Mirroring the Mind: Distilling Human-LikeMetacognitive Strategies into Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>: the judge outputs “answer inclusive” if the reasoning trace identifies the correct answer (or a semantically equivalent answer) as a valid candidate with any supporting fact, context, or logical connection, even if the model later rejects it.
The judge returns “answer exclusive” only when the gold answer is never mentioned, appears solely as part of an unfocused enumeration, or is explicitly framed as a random guess without supporting context.
Importantly, the judge is configured to detect the presence of the correct answer during reasoning, without evaluating reasoning quality.
The full prompt and decision criteria used by the judge are provided in Figure <a href="#A5.F4" title="Figure A4 ‣ E.5 Metacognitive Prompting Prompt ‣ Appendix E Evaluation Prompts ‣ Mirroring the Mind: Distilling Human-LikeMetacognitive Strategies into Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Our analysis reveals two key insights regarding the reliability of these metrics and the nature of reasoning failures.
First, string matching systematically underestimates reasoning in correct samples.
Even when models correctly predicted the final answer, the gold answer string was missing from the reasoning trace in approximately 3–15% of cases (see Tables <a href="#Ax1.T1" title="Table A1 ‣ Appendix ‣ Mirroring the Mind: Distilling Human-LikeMetacognitive Strategies into Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A1</span></a>–<a href="#Ax1.T3" title="Table A3 ‣ Appendix ‣ Mirroring the Mind: Distilling Human-LikeMetacognitive Strategies into Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A3</span></a>).
This result is counter-intuitive: one would expect the correct answer to appear explicitly in the trace.
However, the LLM judge consistently recovers these cases, showing inclusion rates of 98–99%.
This discrepancy indicates that models often rely on internal representations or paraphrasing that strict string matching fails to capture.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Second, incorrect samples often contain the correct answer contextually, but string matching is noisy.
Notably, in incorrect samples, Substring Match often yields higher inclusion rates than the LLM judge (e.g., in Table <a href="#Ax1.T1" title="Table A1 ‣ Appendix ‣ Mirroring the Mind: Distilling Human-LikeMetacognitive Strategies into Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A1</span></a>, 24.72% vs. 14.78%).
This suggests that simple string matching generates false positives by flagging unrelated mentions of the answer entity.
In contrast, the LLM judge is more discerning; it confirms that while models frequently consider the correct answer (answer inclusion) before discarding it, they do not merely hallucinate it randomly.
Together, these findings reinforce our central motivation for MBT.
The prevalence of verified answer-inclusive errors, detected robustly by the LLM judge, suggests that models often possess the correct knowledge but may struggle to preserve it due to insufficient metacognitive monitoring.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Additional Experimental Results</h2>

<figure id="A2.T4" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="A2.T4.8" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table A4</span>: </span><span id="A2.T4.9" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Extended analysis of reasoning stability and efficiency across in-distribution (ID) and out-of-distribution (OOD) benchmarks.
<span id="A2.T4.9.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Degen</span> reports the count of degeneration failures where the model collapsed into repetitive loops (the lower the better).
<span id="A2.T4.9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Length</span> Length metrics report the average output token count across different subsets: (1) <span id="A2.T4.9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Overall</span>: average length of all generated traces; (2) <span id="A2.T4.9.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Correct/Incorrect</span>: average length conditioned on answer correctness; (3) <span id="A2.T4.9.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Valid</span>: average length excluding degenerated samples to reflect the effective reasoning cost of successful generations.
For all metrics, lower values generally indicate higher efficiency and stability.</span></figcaption>
