<section id="A2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.1 </span>Reasoning Efficiency Analysis</h3>

<section id="A2.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">B.1.1 </span>Extended Degeneration and Length Analysis</h4>

<div id="A2.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#A2.T4" title="Table A4 ‣ Appendix B Additional Experimental Results ‣ Mirroring the Mind: Distilling Human-LikeMetacognitive Strategies into Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A4</span></a> provides a granular analysis of reasoning efficiency and structural stability across model scales and benchmarks.
While Table <a href="#S4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ Datasets ‣ 4.1 Experimental Setup ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Mirroring the Mind: Distilling Human-LikeMetacognitive Strategies into Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> focuses on final-task performance, Table <a href="#A2.T4" title="Table A4 ‣ Appendix B Additional Experimental Results ‣ Mirroring the Mind: Distilling Human-LikeMetacognitive Strategies into Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A4</span></a> offers complementary evidence that clarifies how different post-training strategies shape the reasoning process itself.</p>
</div>
<section id="A2.SS1.SSS1.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Reasoning Stability and Degeneration</h5>

<div id="A2.SS1.SSS1.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.SSS1.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Across all model sizes (0.6B–4B) and datasets, MBT-S and MBT-R consistently exhibit near-zero degeneration events, standing in sharp contrast to competing baselines.
Efficiency-oriented methods like TokenSkip <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xia et al., 2025</a>)</cite> and LIMOPro <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xiao et al., 2025</a>)</cite> frequently suffer from reasoning collapse, manifesting as infinite loops or repetition, particularly on the complex OOD benchmark, MuSiQue.
This high rate of degeneration paradoxically inflates the average inference cost, rendering these “efficient” methods impractical for complex multi-hop reasoning.
In contrast, MBT prevents such uncontrolled continuation by internalizing metacognitive regulation, ensuring that the model terminates reasoning effectively even under stress.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS1.SSS1.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Efficiency on Correct Samples: The Metacognitive Overhead</h5>

<div id="A2.SS1.SSS1.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.SSS1.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A closer inspection of output lengths on correct samples reveals a nuanced trade-off.
On simpler in-distribution benchmarks (HotpotQA), pruning-based baselines often yield shorter traces than MBT.
However, the shortness often comes at the cost of stability.
MBT variants, while occasionally producing slightly longer traces on these easier tasks, do so to execute necessary metacognitive behaviors, such as planning, monitoring, and verification.
We interpret this modest increase not as inefficiency, but as intentional metacognitive overhead required to ensure robustness.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS1.SSS1.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Robustness on OOD and Incorrect Samples</h5>

<div id="A2.SS1.SSS1.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.SSS1.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The benefits of this structured approach become decisive on challenging OOD benchmarks.
On MuSiQue dataset, baselines exhibit dramatic length inflation due to reasoning collapse.
For instance, incorrect samples from gpt-oss-distill model often spiral into excessively long, unproductive loops.
MBT substantially mitigates this failure mode; even when the final answer is incorrect, MBT maintains bounded and stable reasoning traces.
This suggests that the injected metacognitive monitoring effectively suppresses runaway exploration, enforcing termination when confidence cannot be established.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A2.T5" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="A2.T5.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table A5</span>: </span><span id="A2.T5.8" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Extended Accuracy-Efficiency Score (AES) analysis across
in-distribution (ID) and out-of-distribution (OOD) benchmarks.
The columns <span id="A2.T5.8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">EM</span>, <span id="A2.T5.8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">F1</span>, and <span id="A2.T5.8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">LLM</span> report the AES values derived using Exact Match, F1 score, and LLM-judge as the accuracy metric (<math id="A2.T5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>Acc), respectively.
AES is normalized relative to the corresponding <span id="A2.T5.8.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Base</span> model (AES = 0).
Positive values indicate an improvement in the accuracy-efficiency trade-off, while negative values indicate a degradation.
</span></figcaption>
<div id="A2.T5.9" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:258.8pt;height:204.6pt;vertical-align:-100.9pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-100.0pt,79.1pt) scale(0.564141530411686,0.564141530411686) ;">
