</figure>
<div id="A2.SS1.SSS1.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.SSS1.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Overall, we demonstrates that MBT achieves a favorable balance between accuracy and efficiency by fundamentally altering how reasoning unfolds.
Rather than relying on heuristic pruning which can compromise stability, MBT improves efficiency by regulating the reasoning process, thereby preventing the structural collapse that plagues standard distillation and pruning methods.
Although this control may introduce a small, deliberate increase in reasoning length on simpler tasks, it consistently yields higher accuracy, as shown in Table <a href="#S4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ Datasets ‣ 4.1 Experimental Setup ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Mirroring the Mind: Distilling Human-LikeMetacognitive Strategies into Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, and significantly improved robustness on more complex benchmarks.
These findings provide concrete evidence that the efficiency gains of MBT arise from improved reasoning regulation, not superficial shortening of reasoning traces.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS1.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">B.1.2 </span>Extended Accuracy-Efficiency-Score Analysis</h4>

<div id="A2.SS1.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#A2.T5" title="Table A5 ‣ Robustness on OOD and Incorrect Samples ‣ B.1.1 Extended Degeneration and Length Analysis ‣ B.1 Reasoning Efficiency Analysis ‣ Appendix B Additional Experimental Results ‣ Mirroring the Mind: Distilling Human-LikeMetacognitive Strategies into Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A5</span></a> reports the Accuracy-Efficiency Score (AES) across model scales (Qwen3-0.6B, 1.7B, and 4B) and benchmarks (HotpotQA, MuSiQue, and 2Wiki).
As defined in Section <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 Evaluation Metrics ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Mirroring the Mind: Distilling Human-LikeMetacognitive Strategies into Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>, AES serves as a composite metric capturing the trade-off between the relative change in output length (<math id="A2.SS1.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>Length) and the relative change in accuracy (<math id="A2.SS1.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>Acc).</p>
</div>
<section id="A2.SS1.SSS2.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Metric-Specific AES Calculation</h5>

<div id="A2.SS1.SSS2.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.SSS2.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Importantly, the columns labeled “EM”, “F1”, and “LLM” in Table <a href="#A2.T5" title="Table A5 ‣ Robustness on OOD and Incorrect Samples ‣ B.1.1 Extended Degeneration and Length Analysis ‣ B.1 Reasoning Efficiency Analysis ‣ Appendix B Additional Experimental Results ‣ Mirroring the Mind: Distilling Human-LikeMetacognitive Strategies into Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A5</span></a> do not represent raw accuracy scores.
Instead, they denote the AES values calculated using the respective metric (Exact Match, F1 score, or LLM-as-a-Judge) as the (<math id="A2.SS1.SSS2.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>Acc) term.
Since AES is normalized against the <span id="A2.SS1.SSS2.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Base</span> model, the <span id="A2.SS1.SSS2.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Base</span> model attains an AES of zero across all configurations by definition.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A2.T6" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="A2.T6.9" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table A6</span>: </span><span id="A2.T6.10" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Extended comparison between naive reasoning distillation
(gpt-oss-distill) and Metacognitive Behavioral Tuning (MBT)
across model scales and benchmarks.
Accuracy metrics (<span id="A2.T6.10.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">EM</span>, <span id="A2.T6.10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">F1</span>, <span id="A2.T6.10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">LLM</span>) are reported alongside stability metrics (<span id="A2.T6.10.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Degen</span>, <span id="A2.T6.10.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Len</span>).
<span id="A2.T6.10.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">AES</span> represents the Accuracy-Efficiency Score computed using the LLM-as-a-Judge metric relative to the Base model.
Note that while distillation achieves high accuracy, it suffers from negative AES due to inefficiency, whereas MBT maintains a positive balance.
</span></figcaption>
