</span></div>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS1.SSS2.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Divergent AES Trajectories: MBT vs. Baselines</h5>

<div id="A2.SS1.SSS2.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.SSS2.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Across all settings, MBT-S and MBT-R consistently achieve positive AES values regardless of the accuracy metric used.
This indicates that MBT improves reasoning accuracy while simultaneously controlling computational cost.
In contrast, efficiency-oriented baselines such as TokenSkip and LIMOPro exhibit strongly negative AES in most configurations, reflecting a severe trade-off where pruning leads to accuracy degradation or instability.
Notably, naive reasoning distillation (gpt-oss-distill) also results in highly negative AES, underscoring that transferring verbose reasoning traces without explicit metacognitive regulation substantially harms efficiency.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS1.SSS2.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Scalability and Generalization</h5>

<div id="A2.SS1.SSS2.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.SSS2.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">While the absolute AES improvements on simpler benchmarks (e.g., HotpotQA) naturally diminish as model scale increases, MBT continues to yield pronounced positive AES on the challenging MuSiQue benchmark, even at the 4B scale.
This pattern suggests that the metacognitive behaviors injected by MBT are particularly effective under high reasoning complexity, where uncontrolled exploration is a major bottleneck.
These results indicate that the observed efficiency gains are robust and generalize across different evaluation protocols.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.2 </span>Extended Comparison between Naive Reasoning Distillation and MBT</h3>

<div id="A2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In Section <a href="#S5.SS1" title="5.1 Is Metacognitive Behavior Injection Reducible to Reasoning Distillation? ‣ 5 Discussion ‣ Mirroring the Mind: Distilling Human-LikeMetacognitive Strategies into Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a>, we examined whether naive reasoning distillation from a stronger teacher model can serve as an effective alternative to explicit metacognitive regulation.
Focusing on the MuSiQue benchmark with a 4B model, we showed that while distillation preserves reasoning accuracy, it induces severe degeneration and bloated output length, ultimately resulting in poor AES.
In contrast, MBT stabilizes the generation process, yielding more efficient and reliable reasoning.
In this section, we extend this analysis to all model scales and benchmarks (Table <a href="#A2.T6" title="Table A6 ‣ Metric-Specific AES Calculation ‣ B.1.2 Extended Accuracy-Efficiency-Score Analysis ‣ B.1 Reasoning Efficiency Analysis ‣ Appendix B Additional Experimental Results ‣ Mirroring the Mind: Distilling Human-LikeMetacognitive Strategies into Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A6</span></a>), providing a comprehensive comparison that clarifies why and when metacognitive regulation is essential.</p>
</div>
<section id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">The Fragility of Naive Distillation</h5>

<div id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in Table <a href="#A2.T6" title="Table A6 ‣ Metric-Specific AES Calculation ‣ B.1.2 Extended Accuracy-Efficiency-Score Analysis ‣ B.1 Reasoning Efficiency Analysis ‣ Appendix B Additional Experimental Results ‣ Mirroring the Mind: Distilling Human-LikeMetacognitive Strategies into Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A6</span></a>, naive reasoning distillation (gpt-oss-distill) consistently struggles with structural instability across all model sizes.
Despite achieving competitive accuracy scores (EM, F1, LLM), these distilled models exhibit a high frequency of degeneration and excessively long traces.
This behavior leads to uniformly negative AES values, indicating that any accuracy gains are negated by substantial inefficiencies.
The instability is particularly acute on challenging OOD benchmarks like MuSiQue, where distilled models fail to terminate reasoning effectively.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">The Impact of Metacognitive Regulation</h5>

<div id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Conversely, both MBT-S and MBT-R drastically curtail degeneration and reasoning length across all settings while preserving accuracy.
MBT thus achieves consistently positive AES values, demonstrating a robust accuracy-efficiency trade-off.
Notably, on MuSiQue, MBT maintains strong positive AES even at the 4B scale.
This highlights that metacognitive behaviors are most effective under high reasoning complexity, where uncontrolled exploration is otherwise prone to occur.
These results reinforce the central claim of our work: simply distilling reasoning traces is insufficient.
Instead, explicitly injecting metacognitive behaviors enables models to regulate when and how to reason, ensuring stability and efficiency across varying scales and distribution shifts.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.3 </span>Hyperparameter Sensitivity of Reasoning Distillation</h3>

<figure id="A2.T7" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="A2.T7.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table A7</span>: </span><span id="A2.T7.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
Hyperparameter sensitivity analysis of naive reasoning distillation (gpt-oss-distill).
We report performance across varying Learning Rates (<span id="A2.T7.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">LR</span>) and Batch Sizes (<span id="A2.T7.7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">BS</span>).
The results show that on complex benchmarks like MuSiQue, the degeneration count (<span id="A2.T7.7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Degen</span>) consistently exceeds 300 regardless of the setting.
This uniformity confirms that reasoning instability is a fundamental characteristic of raw distillation rather than a sensitivity to specific hyperparameters.
</span></figcaption>
