</span></div>
</figure>
<div id="A2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#A2.T7" title="Table A7 ‣ B.3 Hyperparameter Sensitivity of Reasoning Distillation ‣ Appendix B Additional Experimental Results ‣ Mirroring the Mind: Distilling Human-LikeMetacognitive Strategies into Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A7</span></a> reports the performance of naive reasoning distillation (gpt-oss-distill) across a wide range of learning rates (LR) and batch sizes (BS).
The results reveal a critical insight: while varying hyperparameters leads to marginal fluctuations in accuracy, it fails to mitigate the model’s fundamental instability</p>
</div>
<section id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Persistent Instability of Raw Distillation</h5>

<div id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The structural fragility is most evident on the challenging MuSiQue benchmark.
We observe that the degeneration count (Degen) consistently exceeds 300 across nearly all configurations, indicating a pervasive tendency toward reasoning collapse.
Such widespread failure, where the model enters repetitive loops hundreds of times, confirms that the observed inefficiency is not a byproduct of suboptimal hyperparameter tuning.
Instead, it demonstrates that raw reasoning distillation inherently lacks the regulatory mechanism to control exploration, rendering it unstable regardless of the specific training settings.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Final Configuration Selection</h5>

<div id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Based on this analysis, we adopt LR <math id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="=1\times 10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">=1\times 10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math> and BS <math id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="=128" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mn>128</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">=128</annotation></semantics></math> as the standard distillation setting for this paper.
This configuration achieves near-peak accuracy among the tested variants while avoiding the extreme degradation observed at excessively high learning rates.
Furthermore, aligning these parameters with the MBT training configuration ensures a strictly controlled and fair comparison across all methods.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.4 </span>Impact of Behavior Injection Strategies</h3>

<figure id="A2.F1" class="ltx_figure">
<div id="A2.F1.3" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:345.0pt;height:85.4pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-1.7pt,0.4pt) scale(0.990430636471552,0.990430636471552) ;"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.22508v1/compare_MBI_bar.svg" id="A2.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/119;" width="476" height="119"></object>
</span></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A2.F1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure A1</span>: </span><span id="A2.F1.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
Comparison of Accuracy-Efficiency Score (AES) across behavior injection strategies on the MuSiQue dataset.
While rewriting-based baselines (Direct-R, Distill-R) improve over Prompting, MBT-S and MBT-R achieve the highest AES.
The result demonstrates that explicitly synthesizing or rewriting traces tailored to the student’s learning needs (MBT) is more effective than simply rewriting external teacher traces.
</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A2.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we analyze how different sources of reasoning traces affect the efficacy of behavior injection.
To isolate the impact of our proposed strategies, we compare MBT against the following baselines:</p>
<ul id="A2.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Metacognitive Prompting (Prompt)</span>: Induces metacognitive behaviors via system prompts at inference time without parameter updates.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Direct-R</span>: Rewrites reasoning traces generated by the teacher model.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Distill-R</span>: Rewrites reasoning traces produced by the distilled model, which was trained on the teacher model’s raw reasoning traces, and subsequently uses these rewritten traces to fine-tune the base model.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<section id="A2.SS4.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Analysis of Accuracy-Efficiency Score (AES)</h5>

<div id="A2.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#A2.F1" title="Figure A1 ‣ B.4 Impact of Behavior Injection Strategies ‣ Appendix B Additional Experimental Results ‣ Mirroring the Mind: Distilling Human-LikeMetacognitive Strategies into Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A1</span></a> reports the AES across these methods.
First, the Metacognitive Prompting baseline yields limited improvements and even negative AES, confirming that inference-time intervention alone is insufficient to internalize stable control.
Second, while rewriting-based baselines (Direct-R and Distill-R) achieve significant gains over prompting, they are consistently outperformed by MBT-S and MBT-R.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS4.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Interpreting the Performance Gap</h5>

<div id="A2.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This performance gap underscores the importance of data alignment.
MBT-S establishes a strong baseline by synthesizing rigorous reasoning exemplars from scratch, while MBT-R directly optimizes the student’s policy by refining on-policy traces.
In contrast, Direct-R and Distill-R rely on external traces (from teacher or distilled models), which may introduce a distribution mismatch or fail to address the specific reasoning deficits of the student model.
Therefore, MBT’s approach of constructing highly structured synthetic data or correcting the model’s own errors proves most effective for internalizing metacognitive strategies.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.5 </span>Detailed Analysis of Overthinking, Underthinking, and Metacognitive Behavior</h3>

<div id="A2.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section complements the visual summaries in Figures <a href="#S5.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 5.1 Is Metacognitive Behavior Injection Reducible to Reasoning Distillation? ‣ 5 Discussion ‣ Mirroring the Mind: Distilling Human-LikeMetacognitive Strategies into Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> and <a href="#S5.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 5.3 Does MBT Effectively Inject Metacognitive Behaviors? ‣ 5 Discussion ‣ Mirroring the Mind: Distilling Human-LikeMetacognitive Strategies into Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> by providing a granular quantitative analysis of reasoning stability.
We employ the behavioral metrics defined in Section <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 Evaluation Metrics ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Mirroring the Mind: Distilling Human-LikeMetacognitive Strategies into Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>, namely Overthinking (<math id="A2.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{\text{OT}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mtext>OT</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{\text{OT}}</annotation></semantics></math>), Underthinking (<math id="A2.SS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{\text{UT}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mtext>UT</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{\text{UT}}</annotation></semantics></math>), and the Metacognition Score, to characterize how effectively different methods regulate the reasoning process beyond mere task accuracy.</p>
</div>
<section id="A2.SS5.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Quantifying Reasoning Instability</h5>

<div id="A2.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We introduce two specialized metrics to detect structural stagnation, computed using an LLM-as-a-Judge (see Figures <a href="#A5.F7" title="Figure A7 ‣ E.5 Metacognitive Prompting Prompt ‣ Appendix E Evaluation Prompts ‣ Mirroring the Mind: Distilling Human-LikeMetacognitive Strategies into Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A7</span></a> and <a href="#A5.F8" title="Figure A8 ‣ E.5 Metacognitive Prompting Prompt ‣ Appendix E Evaluation Prompts ‣ Mirroring the Mind: Distilling Human-LikeMetacognitive Strategies into Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A8</span></a> for the full prompts).</p>
<ul id="A2.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A2.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Overthinking</span> (<math id="A2.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{\text{OT}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mtext>OT</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{\text{OT}}</annotation></semantics></math>) measures post-solution stagnation in correct samples. It captures cases where the model, despite having derived the answer, fails to terminate and devolves into redundant verification or circular doubt.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Underthinking</span> (<math id="A2.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{\text{UT}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mtext>UT</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{\text{UT}}</annotation></semantics></math>) measures pre-solution stagnation in incorrect samples.
It identifies traces that fail to progress due to repetitive looping or premature termination.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="A2.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">In both cases, the judge detects the onset of stagnation and quantifies the normalized gap between the full trace length and the effective reasoning length; thus, lower scores indicate higher structural efficiency.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS5.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Quantifying Metacognitive Quality</h5>

<div id="A2.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Complementing these stability metrics, the <span id="A2.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Metacognition Score</span> (0–5) evaluates the explicit presence of regulatory behaviors (such as goal filtering, planning, monitoring, and self-correction) independent of the final answer’s correctness.
This provides a direct measure of how well the model adheres to the intended reasoning structure.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS5.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Impact of Metacognitive Regulation on Stability</h5>

<div id="A2.SS5.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS5.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#A2.T8" title="Table A8 ‣ Distinct Behavioral Profiles of MBT Variants ‣ B.5 Detailed Analysis of Overthinking, Underthinking, and Metacognitive Behavior ‣ Appendix B Additional Experimental Results ‣ Mirroring the Mind: Distilling Human-LikeMetacognitive Strategies into Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A8</span></a> reports these behavioral scores across model scales (0.6B–4B) and methods.
Our analysis reveals that standard baselines (Prompting, GRPO, RS) and efficiency-oriented methods (TokenSkip, LIMOPro) consistently exhibit high levels of either overthinking or underthinking.
This confirms that relying on implicit heuristics or surface-level constraints is insufficient to regulate when to continue and when to terminate reasoning.
In contrast, both MBT-S and MBT-R dramatically reduce instability scores (<math id="A2.SS5.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{\text{OT}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mtext>OT</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{\text{OT}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS5.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{\text{UT}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mtext>UT</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{\text{UT}}</annotation></semantics></math>) while achieving the highest Metacognition Scores.
This stabilization pattern holds consistent across all model scales, demonstrating that explicit metacognitive regulation is a generalizable capability rather than an artifact of larger model capacity.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS5.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Distinct Behavioral Profiles of MBT Variants</h5>

<div id="A2.SS5.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS5.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Interestingly, we observe a slight behavioral divergence between the two proposed variants.
MBT-S tends to be more aggressive in suppressing excessive continuation (lowest <math id="A2.SS5.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{\text{OT}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mtext>OT</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{\text{OT}}</annotation></semantics></math>), reflecting the highly optimized nature of its synthetic training data.
Meanwhile, MBT-R attains slightly higher Metacognition Scores, suggesting that the process of correcting one’s own errors fosters a richer display of explicit reflection.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS5.SSS0.Px4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS5.SSS0.Px4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Overall, these results provide concrete behavioral evidence that MBT does not merely optimize abstract metacognitive signals.
Instead, it successfully translates metacognitive supervision into actionable control, enabling the model to autonomously monitor its progress, correct errors, and terminate efficiently.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A2.T8" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="A2.T8.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table A8</span>: </span><span id="A2.T8.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
Quantitative analysis of reasoning stability and metacognitive quality on the MuSiQue dataset.
We report Overthinking (<math id="A2.T8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{\text{OT}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">OT</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{\text{OT}}</annotation></semantics></math>) and Underthinking (<math id="A2.T8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{\text{UT}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">UT</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{\text{UT}}</annotation></semantics></math>) scores to measure reasoning stagnation (the lower the better), alongside the Metacognition Score (<span id="A2.T8.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Meta</span>) which evaluates the richness of self-regulation (the higher the better).
The results demonstrate that MBT variants uniquely achieve minimal instability while maximizing metacognitive quality compared to all baselines.
</span></figcaption>
