<td id="A2.T9.7.1.23.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="A2.T9.7.1.23.5.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#F2F2F2;">61.56</span></td>
<td id="A2.T9.7.1.23.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="A2.T9.7.1.23.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-bg-color:#F2F2F2;">63.59</span></td>
<td id="A2.T9.7.1.23.7" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="A2.T9.7.1.23.7.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#F2F2F2;">1</span></td>
<td id="A2.T9.7.1.23.8" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_r"><span id="A2.T9.7.1.23.8.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#F2F2F2;">739</span></td>
<td id="A2.T9.7.1.23.9" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="A2.T9.7.1.23.9.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#F2F2F2;">68.26</span></td>
<td id="A2.T9.7.1.23.10" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="A2.T9.7.1.23.10.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-bg-color:#F2F2F2;">77.92</span></td>
<td id="A2.T9.7.1.23.11" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="A2.T9.7.1.23.11.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#F2F2F2;">85.42</span></td>
<td id="A2.T9.7.1.23.12" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="A2.T9.7.1.23.12.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-bg-color:#F2F2F2;">0</span></td>
<td id="A2.T9.7.1.23.13" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_r"><span id="A2.T9.7.1.23.13.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#F2F2F2;">664</span></td>
<td id="A2.T9.7.1.23.14" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="A2.T9.7.1.23.14.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#F2F2F2;">68.22</span></td>
<td id="A2.T9.7.1.23.15" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="A2.T9.7.1.23.15.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#F2F2F2;">82.06</span></td>
<td id="A2.T9.7.1.23.16" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="A2.T9.7.1.23.16.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#F2F2F2;">89.29</span></td>
<td id="A2.T9.7.1.23.17" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="A2.T9.7.1.23.17.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-bg-color:#F2F2F2;">0</span></td>
<td id="A2.T9.7.1.23.18" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="A2.T9.7.1.23.18.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#F2F2F2;">641</span></td></tr>
</table>
</span></div>
</figure>
<div id="A2.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section provides a detailed ablation analysis of the MBT framework to disentangle the specific contributions of Supervised Fine-Tuning (SFT) and Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO).
Table <a href="#A2.T9" title="Table A9 ‣ B.6 Ablation Study: Disentangling SFT and GRPO ‣ Appendix B Additional Experimental Results ‣ Mirroring the Mind: Distilling Human-LikeMetacognitive Strategies into Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A9</span></a> summarizes the performance across four configurations: Base model, GRPO-only, SFT-only (MBT-S/R), and the full MBT pipeline (SFT+GRPO).</p>
</div>
<section id="A2.SS6.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">The Risk of Unstructured Reinforcement (GRPO-only)</h5>

<div id="A2.SS6.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS6.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Applying GRPO directly to the base model consistently improves accuracy metrics (EM, F1) across all scales.
However, these gains come at a severe cost: GRPO-only models exhibit dramatically longer outputs and high degeneration rates.
This indicates that reinforcement learning without prior structural guidance tends to amplify exploration blindly, leading to unstable and excessive reasoning behaviors.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS6.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">SFT as a Stabilizing Prior (SFT-only)</h5>

<div id="A2.SS6.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS6.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In contrast, SFT using MBT-generated traces yields notable accuracy improvements while substantially reducing degeneration and reasoning length compared to GRPO-only baselines.
Even without reinforcement learning, SFT effectively internalizes high-level reasoning discipline, stabilizing the process and mitigating overthinking.
Comparing the variants, MBT-S produces tighter, more concise traces due to its synthetic nature, whereas MBT-R preserves richer intermediate deductions from the original distribution of the student model.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS6.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">The Synergy of Structure and Refinement (SFT+GRPO)</h5>

<div id="A2.SS6.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS6.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The most consistent improvements are observed when SFT and GRPO are combined.
In the full MBT configuration, GRPO operates on an already stabilized policy, allowing it to further refine performance without reintroducing instability.
Across all scales, this two-stage approach achieves the optimal balance between accuracy, efficiency, and stability.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS6.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS6.SSS0.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">These findings highlight a clear division of labor: SFT establishes the metacognitive structure (learning how to reason), while GRPO refines the policy (learning what generates rewards).
The result confirms that naively applying RL leads to structural collapse, whereas MBT ensures robust and efficient generalization.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>Experimental Details</h2>

<section id="A3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.1 </span>Post-training via Group Relative Policy Optimization</h3>

<div id="A3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">After SFT, we further refine the model using Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shao et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.
We employ GRPO as our reinforcement learning algorithm due to its stability and efficiency in optimizing reasoning models.</p>
</div>
<section id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Group Relative Policy Optimization</h5>

<div id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Unlike standard RL methods that require a separate value function (critic), Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO) optimizes the policy by estimating advantages relatively within a group of sampled outputs, thereby eliminating the need for a critic.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Specifically, for each query <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>, GRPO samples a group of <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> outputs <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{o_{1},\dots,o_{G}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{o_{1},\dots,o_{G}\}</annotation></semantics></math> from the current policy and updates the model based on their relative reward within the group.
The estimated advantage <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{A}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{A}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for the <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th output is computed via group-wise normalization as follows:</p>
<table id="A3.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{A}_{i}=\frac{r_{i}-\mathrm{mean}(\{r_{1},\dots,r_{G}\})}{\mathrm{std}(\{r_{1},\dots,r_{G}\})}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>mean</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>std</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{A}_{i}=\frac{r_{i}-\mathrm{mean}(\{r_{1},\dots,r_{G}\})}{\mathrm{std}(\{r_{1},\dots,r_{G}\})}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">In our framework, we employ an outcome-based reward <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> defined as the F1 score between the predicted answer <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{a}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{a}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and the corresponding gold answer <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>.
This reward provides a soft and dense training signal that captures partial correctness through textual overlap, offering finer granularity than binary accuracy.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">GRPO optimizes a training objective defined as:</p>
<table id="A3.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{GRPO}}(\theta)=-\mathbb{E}_{q\sim\mathcal{D},\{o_{i}\}_{i=1}^{G}\sim\pi_{\theta_{\mathrm{old}}}}\Bigg[\frac{1}{G}\sum_{i=1}^{G}\sum_{t=1}^{|o_{i}|}\min\Big(\rho_{i,t}(\theta)\hat{A}_{i},\;\mathrm{clip}\big(\rho_{i,t}(\theta),1-\epsilon,1+\epsilon\big)\hat{A}_{i}\Big)-\beta\,\mathbb{D}_{\mathrm{KL}}(\pi_{\theta}\|\pi_{\mathrm{ref}})\Bigg]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>GRPO</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>G</mi></msubsup><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>old</mi></msub></msub></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">[</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>G</mi></mfrac><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>G</mi></munderover><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></munderover><mi>min</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mi>clip</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi><msub><mi>𝔻</mi><mi>KL</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>ref</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{GRPO}}(\theta)=-\mathbb{E}_{q\sim\mathcal{D},\{o_{i}\}_{i=1}^{G}\sim\pi_{\theta_{\mathrm{old}}}}\Bigg[\frac{1}{G}\sum_{i=1}^{G}\sum_{t=1}^{|o_{i}|}\min\Big(\rho_{i,t}(\theta)\hat{A}_{i},\;\mathrm{clip}\big(\rho_{i,t}(\theta),1-\epsilon,1+\epsilon\big)\hat{A}_{i}\Big)-\beta\,\mathbb{D}_{\mathrm{KL}}(\pi_{\theta}\|\pi_{\mathrm{ref}})\Bigg],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="A3.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{i,t}(\theta)=\frac{\pi_{\theta}\big(o_{i,t}\mid q,o_{i,&lt;t}\big)}{\pi_{\theta_{\mathrm{old}}}\big(o_{i,t}\mid q,o_{i,&lt;t}\big)}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>old</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{i,t}(\theta)=\frac{\pi_{\theta}\big(o_{i,t}\mid q,o_{i,&lt;t}\big)}{\pi_{\theta_{\mathrm{old}}}\big(o_{i,t}\mid q,o_{i,&lt;t}\big)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{i,t}(\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{i,t}(\theta)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the importance sampling ratio between the current policy <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> and the behavior policy <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta_{\mathrm{old}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>old</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta_{\mathrm{old}}}</annotation></semantics></math> at the the <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>-th token of the <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th generated output.
Since the preceding SFT stage has already refined the exploration space with structured metacognitive traces, GRPO can effectively leverage these F1 score-based outcome signals to reinforce the most efficient and robust reasoning paths.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Training setup</h5>

<div id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We implement all GRPO experiments using the verl framework <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sheng et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. We train the target model, Qwen3, on the HotpotQA training set for 60 steps. We use a training batch size of 512 and set the maximum response length to 4096 tokens.
We use a learning rate of <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\times 10^{-6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\times 10^{-6}</annotation></semantics></math>, a weight decay of <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.1</annotation></semantics></math>, and a KL regularization coefficient <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=1\times 10^{-3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=1\times 10^{-3}</annotation></semantics></math>. All experiments are conducted using 4 H100 GPUs.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.2 </span>Baseline Methods</h3>

<div id="A3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the main paper, we compare MBT against representative efficient reasoning baselines, TokenSkip <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xia et al., 2025</a>)</cite>and LIMOPro <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xiao et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.
Both approaches aim to produce compact reasoning traces by selectively retaining important components from model-generated reasoning and then fine-tuning a target model via supervised fine-tuning (SFT) so that it learns to reason more efficiently.
Unlike MBT, these approaches focus on compressing or pruning existing reasoning traces rather than stabilizing intermediate reasoning through metacognitive control.</p>
</div>
<section id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">TokenSkip</h5>

<div id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">TokenSkip first obtains full reasoning traces from a target model. It then estimates token-level importance scores using a bidirectional BERT-based model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xia et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pan et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.
Based on these scores, unimportant tokens are pruned to construct compact reasoning traces, which are subsequently used as SFT data.
Following the TokenSkip, we generate reasoning traces on the HotpotQA training set using the target model Qwen3 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.
We filter samples based on answer correctness and randomly sample a compression ratio <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> for each training instance from the set {0.5,0.6,0.7,0.8,0.9,1.0}.
We prune tokens based on the sampled compression ratio and use the resulting compressed reasoning traces as the SFT dataset for training.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">LIMOPro</h5>

<div id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">LIMOPro leverages reasoning traces generated by a model that is more powerful than the target model.
It focuses on pruning functional reasoning components that do not contribute to logical progression, using a perplexity-based importance score.
Specifically, LIMOPro first segments the entire reasoning trace into intermediate steps using an LLM.
It then classifies each step as either logical progression or functional (e.g., validation or error correction).
For steps in the functional group, LIMOPro computes importance scores based on the change in perplexity when removing each step.
Based on these scores, it prunes steps with lower importance and uses the resulting compressed reasoning traces as SFT data.
In our experiments, we generate reasoning traces on the HotpotQA training set using gpt-oss-120B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Agarwal et al., 2025b</a>)</cite>.
We use the same model for step segmentation and step classification.
Following the official LIMOPro implementation, we filter samples based on answer correctness, apply a pruning ratio of 0.5, and use the pruned reasoning traces to construct the SFT dataset for training.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Training setup</h5>

<div id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For all baseline SFT experiments, we train the target model, Qwen3, for 1 epoch with a learning rate of <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\times 10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\times 10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math> and a batch size of 128. We employ a cosine learning rate scheduler with a warmup ratio of 0.1. The optimizer is configured with <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{1}=0.9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{1}=0.9</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{2}=0.999" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.999</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{2}=0.999</annotation></semantics></math>, a weight decay of 0.0, and a maximum gradient norm of 0.3. All experiments are conducted using 4 L40 GPUs or 4 H100 GPUs.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A4" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix D </span>Qualitative Analysis of Metacognitive Reasoning Traces</h2>

<div id="A4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we qualitatively examine how metacognitive behaviors manifest in reasoning traces before and after applying MBT.
We present three representative cases in sequence: an initial reasoning trace generated without metacognitive control, a normatively structured trace synthesized by MBT-S, and a rewritten trace by MBT-R.
This progression illustrates how metacognitive structure first stabilizes reasoning and subsequently enables the explicit detection and repair of logical failures.</p>
</div>
<section id="A4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.1 </span>Initial Reasoning Trace: Uncontrolled and Unstable Reasoning</h3>

<div id="A4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#A5.F2" title="Figure A2 ‣ E.5 Metacognitive Prompting Prompt ‣ Appendix E Evaluation Prompts ‣ Mirroring the Mind: Distilling Human-LikeMetacognitive Strategies into Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A2</span></a> presents a representative example of an initial reasoning trace generated by the base Qwen3 model.
This trace exemplifies a typical failure mode observed in LRMs when explicit metacognitive control is absent.
Although the document set contains sufficient evidence to answer the question, the model introduces an unverified assumption early in the process and commits to it without subsequent monitoring.
Furthermore, despite repeated expressions of uncertainty, the model fails to trigger hypothesis revision, ultimately terminating with an incorrect conclusion due to a misjudgment of logical sufficiency.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.2 </span>MBT-S: Metacognitively Structured Reasoning Trace by Synthesis</h3>

<div id="A4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#A5.F3.sf1" title="Figure 3(a) ‣ E.5 Metacognitive Prompting Prompt ‣ Appendix E Evaluation Prompts ‣ Mirroring the Mind: Distilling Human-LikeMetacognitive Strategies into Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3(a)</span></a> presents a representative example of a metacognitively structured reasoning trace (MBT-S) synthesized by gpt-oss-120B.
Unlike the uncontrolled baseline, the MBT-S trace adheres to a normative structure, explicitly organizing the process into distinct metacognitive phases: task clarification, planning, controlled evidence integration, self-correction, and verification.
By establishing a rigorous logical framework from the outset, this approach ensures a coherent trajectory, effectively preventing premature commitment to unsupported assumptions and guiding the model toward the correct solution.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.3 </span>MBT-R: Metacognitively Structured Reasoning Trace by Rewriting</h3>

<div id="A4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#A5.F3.sf2" title="Figure 3(b) ‣ E.5 Metacognitive Prompting Prompt ‣ Appendix E Evaluation Prompts ‣ Mirroring the Mind: Distilling Human-LikeMetacognitive Strategies into Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3(b)</span></a> illustrates the corresponding MBT-R trace, generated by rewriting the flawed initial trace shown in Figure <a href="#A5.F2" title="Figure A2 ‣ E.5 Metacognitive Prompting Prompt ‣ Appendix E Evaluation Prompts ‣ Mirroring the Mind: Distilling Human-LikeMetacognitive Strategies into Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A2</span></a>.
In this process, the teacher model revisits the student’s intermediate assumptions and explicitly re-examines their validity against the provided documents.
The rewriting procedure does not discard the original trace wholesale; instead, it preserves consistent factual observations while revising only those steps dependent on unsupported inferences.
This yields a refined reasoning trajectory in which intermediate conclusions are rigorously validated, culminating in a final answer derived through active self-correction and verification.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A5" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix E </span>Evaluation Prompts</h2>

<div id="A5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we describe the evaluation prompts used throughout the paper to analyze various behavioral aspects of model reasoning.
Specifically, we present (i) a set of evaluation prompts executed by gpt-oss-120B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Agarwal et al., 2025b</a>)</cite> acting as an external judge to quantify reasoning behaviors, and (ii) the inference prompts provided to the target model, Qwen3 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, for the Base and Metacognitive Prompting baselines.
Rather than relying on a single notion of correctness, the evaluation framework decomposes reasoning behavior into multiple complementary dimensions, each defined by a dedicated prompt.</p>
</div>
<section id="A5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">E.1 </span>Answer Inclusion Evaluation Prompt</h3>

<div id="A5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The answer inclusion prompt (Figure <a href="#A5.F4" title="Figure A4 ‣ E.5 Metacognitive Prompting Prompt ‣ Appendix E Evaluation Prompts ‣ Mirroring the Mind: Distilling Human-LikeMetacognitive Strategies into Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A4</span></a>) determines whether a reasoning trace is answer-inclusive, i.e., whether the correct answer is derived, inferred, or identified at any point with supporting context.
The evaluation does not assess final-answer correctness or the reasoning quality.
Instead, it applies a lenient criterion that prioritizes detecting the presence of correct intermediate conclusions, even if the model later rejects them.
Conversely, the judge explicitly excludes cases where the correct answer appears only as part of an unfocused enumeration or as a random guess.
The prompt underlines the answer-inclusive error analysis presented in Figure <a href="#S1.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Mirroring the Mind: Distilling Human-LikeMetacognitive Strategies into Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> and the quantitative results in Tables <a href="#Ax1.T1" title="Table A1 ‣ Appendix ‣ Mirroring the Mind: Distilling Human-LikeMetacognitive Strategies into Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A1</span></a>–<a href="#Ax1.T3" title="Table A3 ‣ Appendix ‣ Mirroring the Mind: Distilling Human-LikeMetacognitive Strategies into Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">E.2 </span>Metacognitively Grounded Reasoning Trace Prompt</h3>

<section id="A5.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">E.2.1 </span>MBT-S: Metacognitive Trace Synthesis</h4>

<div id="A5.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The synthesis prompt used for MBT-S (Figure <a href="#A5.F5" title="Figure A5 ‣ E.5 Metacognitive Prompting Prompt ‣ Appendix E Evaluation Prompts ‣ Mirroring the Mind: Distilling Human-LikeMetacognitive Strategies into Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A5</span></a>) instructs the teacher model (gpt-oss-120B) to generate reasoning traces from scratch.
Given a query, contextual documents, and the gold answer, the prompt enforces a rigorous five-phase process: goal clarification, planning, execution monitoring, self-correction, and verification.
Although the gold answer is provided as input, the model is instructed to simulate an independent discovery process without referencing the answer key.
The resulting traces serve as high-quality supervision for MBT-S, enabling the student model to internalize explicit metacognitive structures.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">E.2.2 </span>MBT-R: Metacognitive Trace Rewriting</h4>

<div id="A5.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The rewriting prompt (Figure <a href="#A5.F6" title="Figure A6 ‣ E.5 Metacognitive Prompting Prompt ‣ Appendix E Evaluation Prompts ‣ Mirroring the Mind: Distilling Human-LikeMetacognitive Strategies into Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A6</span></a>) guides the teacher model (gpt-oss-120B) to refine an existing reasoning trace generated by the student model.
Unlike synthesis, which starts from scratch, this prompt focuses on reorganization.
It explicitly instructs the teacher to preserve valid intermediate deductions while injecting metacognitive behaviors such as monitoring, self-correction, and verification.
When the original trace contains errors, the prompt simulates a realization of error, directing the model to identify the flaw and naturally redirect the trajectory toward the correct solution.
This design ensures that MBT-R stabilizes the initial reasoning traces while retaining informative intermediate steps.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">E.3 </span>Behavioral Evaluation Prompt</h3>

<section id="A5.SS3.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">E.3.1 </span>Overthinking Score</h4>

<div id="A5.SS3.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS3.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The overthinking evaluation prompt (Figure <a href="#A5.F7" title="Figure A7 ‣ E.5 Metacognitive Prompting Prompt ‣ Appendix E Evaluation Prompts ‣ Mirroring the Mind: Distilling Human-LikeMetacognitive Strategies into Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A7</span></a>) is designed to detect post-solution stagnation, a state in which the model continues to generate tokens after the problem has been effectively resolved.
The judge model identifies the precise transition point where necessary verification devolves into unproductive redundancy.
We define overthinking to include repetitive confirmation, baseless doubt without concrete rationale, and circular looping that yields no information gain.
The judge truncates the trace immediately prior to this behavior, and the cut-off point is used to compute the overthinking score (<math id="A5.SS3.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{OT}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{OT}</annotation></semantics></math>) reported in Section <a href="#S5.SS2" title="5.2 Does MBT Mitigate the Structural Failures of Overthinking and Underthinking? ‣ 5 Discussion ‣ Mirroring the Mind: Distilling Human-LikeMetacognitive Strategies into Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS3.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">E.3.2 </span>Underthinking Score</h4>

<div id="A5.SS3.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS3.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Complementing the overthinking score, the underthinking evaluation prompt (Figure <a href="#A5.F8" title="Figure A8 ‣ E.5 Metacognitive Prompting Prompt ‣ Appendix E Evaluation Prompts ‣ Mirroring the Mind: Distilling Human-LikeMetacognitive Strategies into Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A8</span></a>) addresses pre-solution stagnation, where the reasoning process fails to advance toward a conclusion.
The evaluator detects the onset of structural collapse, such as repetitive questioning or looping over previously rejected hypotheses.
Notably, this metric is decoupled from correctness.
If a reasoning trace remains progressive and logically distinct, even when it is factually flawed or leads to an incorrect answer, it is not penalized as underthinking.
The trace is truncated only when structural stagnation occurs, and this point determines the underthinking score (<math id="A5.SS3.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{UT}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{UT}</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS3.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">E.3.3 </span>Metacognition Score</h4>

<div id="A5.SS3.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS3.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The metacognition score evaluation prompt (Figure <a href="#A5.F9" title="Figure A9 ‣ E.5 Metacognitive Prompting Prompt ‣ Appendix E Evaluation Prompts ‣ Mirroring the Mind: Distilling Human-LikeMetacognitive Strategies into Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A9</span></a>) serves as a quantitative instrument to assess the depth of self-regulation.
Utilizing gpt-oss-120B model as the judge, this metric assigns a discrete score (0–5) based on the explicit manifestation of the five core phases: understanding, planning, monitoring, self-correction, and verification.
Lower scores reflect intuitive or strictly linear reasoning, whereas higher scores indicate a sophisticated, actively monitored trajectory.
By outputting a single integer, this prompt provides a rigorous process-level measure of metacognitive quality, complementing the binary and truncation-based stability metrics.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">E.4 </span>Base Prompt</h3>

<div id="A5.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For the Base model evaluation, we employ a minimal multi-hop question answering prompt (Figure <a href="#A5.F10" title="Figure A10 ‣ E.5 Metacognitive Prompting Prompt ‣ Appendix E Evaluation Prompts ‣ Mirroring the Mind: Distilling Human-LikeMetacognitive Strategies into Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A10</span></a>) that requests only the final answer based on retrieved documents.
This prompt imposes no structural constraints, allowing the model to rely solely on its internal inductive biases.
The unconstrained setting serves as a control to measure the model’s native reasoning tendencies without external behavioral guidance.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">E.5 </span>Metacognitive Prompting Prompt</h3>

<div id="A5.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The Metacognitive Prompting baseline augments the <span id="A5.SS5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Base</span> setting by injecting explicit metacognitive instructions at inference time, without any additional post-training (Figure <a href="#A5.F11" title="Figure A11 ‣ E.5 Metacognitive Prompting Prompt ‣ Appendix E Evaluation Prompts ‣ Mirroring the Mind: Distilling Human-LikeMetacognitive Strategies into Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A11</span></a>).
Unlike MBT, which internalizes strategies through parameter updates, this setting relies exclusively on a system prompt to enforce the five-phase reasoning structure.
By injecting explicit instructions for goal clarification, planning, and self-correction, this baseline allows us to disentangle the impact of external guidance from that of internalized capability.
This comparison is instrumental to determining whether stable reasoning requires fundamental behavioral tuning or merely strong instruction following.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A5.F2" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.22508v1/Initial_sample_qualitative.svg" id="A5.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:381/364;" width="381" height="364"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A5.F2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure A2</span>: </span><span id="A5.F2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
Representative example of an initial reasoning trace generated by the base model.
The model introduces an unverified assumption early in the process and fails to trigger self-correction despite repeated uncertainty, ultimately leading to an incorrect conclusion.
This illustrates a typical structural failure due to the lack of metacognitive regulation.
Best viewed in color.
</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
<figure id="A5.SS5.fig1" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="A5.F3.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.22508v1/MBT-S_qualitative.svg" id="A5.F3.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:367/242;" width="367" height="242"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A5.F3.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="A5.F3.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Metacognitively structured reasoning trace synthesized by MBT-S.
Unlike the baseline, the trace follows a normative structure with explicit phases for planning, execution, and verification.
By establishing a rigorous logical framework from the outset, the model effectively prevents premature commitment to false assumptions and derives the correct solution. Best viewed in color.
</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="A5.F3.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.22508v1/MBT-R_qualitative.svg" id="A5.F3.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:367/333;" width="367" height="333"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A5.F3.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="A5.F3.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Metacognitively structured reasoning trace refined by MBT-R.
The trace is generated by rewriting the flawed initial trace from Figure <a href="#A5.F2" title="Figure A2 ‣ E.5 Metacognitive Prompting Prompt ‣ Appendix E Evaluation Prompts ‣ Mirroring the Mind: Distilling Human-LikeMetacognitive Strategies into Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A2</span></a>.
The model explicitly detects the unsupported inference in the original reasoning and corrects the logical path while preserving valid intermediate steps, demonstrating the capability for self-correction. Best viewed in color.
</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
</figure>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
<figure id="A5.F4" class="ltx_figure"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="A5.F4.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_centering" height="637.62" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 477.38 637.62" width="477.38"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,637.62) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 5.91 L 0 619.58 C 0 622.84 2.64 625.48 5.91 625.48 L 471.47 625.48 C 474.73 625.48 477.38 622.84 477.38 619.58 L 477.38 5.91 C 477.38 2.64 474.73 0 471.47 0 L 5.91 0 C 2.64 0 0 2.64 0 5.91 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F0F0FF;" fill="#F0F0FF" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.97 5.91 L 1.97 619.58 C 1.97 621.75 3.73 623.51 5.91 623.51 L 471.47 623.51 C 473.65 623.51 475.41 621.75 475.41 619.58 L 475.41 5.91 C 475.41 3.73 473.65 1.97 471.47 1.97 L 5.91 1.97 C 3.73 1.97 1.97 3.73 1.97 5.91 Z"></path></g><g transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 15 615.48)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_nestedsvg" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#FFFFFF;" fill="#FFFFFF" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 2.95 L 0 19.19 C 0 20.82 1.32 22.14 2.95 22.14 L 369.44 22.14 C 371.07 22.14 372.39 20.82 372.39 19.19 L 372.39 2.95 C 372.39 1.32 371.07 0 369.44 0 L 2.95 0 C 1.32 0 0 1.32 0 2.95 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 2.95 L 0 19.19 C 0 20.82 1.32 22.14 2.95 22.14 L 369.44 22.14 C 371.07 22.14 372.39 20.82 372.39 19.19 L 372.39 2.95 C 372.39 1.32 371.07 0 369.44 0 L 2.95 0 C 1.32 0 0 1.32 0 2.95 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 11.81 7.61)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:25.29em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="12.3" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.61)" width="349.93"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="A5.F4.pic1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;">LLM-as-a-Judge Prompt for Answer Inclusion Evaluation</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 21.65 12.13)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:31.37em;--ltx-fo-height:43.2em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.25em;font-size:10pt;" height="601.22" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 597.77)" width="434.07"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="A5.F4.pic1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:31.37em;">
<span id="A5.F4.pic1.2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.F4.pic1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">{
<br class="ltx_break">  “role”: “user”,
<br class="ltx_break">   “content”: “““
<br class="ltx_break">You are an expert evaluator. Your task is to check if the draft solution contains any instance where the correct answer is derived, inferred, or identified with some supporting context.</span></span>
<span id="A5.F4.pic1.2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.F4.pic1.2.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Please apply a lenient standard for reasoning. You should prioritize detecting the correct answer over critiquing the quality of the logic.</span></span>
<span id="A5.F4.pic1.2.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.F4.pic1.2.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Criteria for outputting YES:</span></span>
<span id="A5.F4.pic1.2.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.F4.pic1.2.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">- Output YES if the draft solution identifies the correct answer (or a semantically equivalent answer) as a valid
<br class="ltx_break">   candidate at any point.</span></span>
<span id="A5.F4.pic1.2.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.F4.pic1.2.5.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">- Output YES if the correct answer appears along with a supporting fact, context, or logical connection.</span></span>
<span id="A5.F4.pic1.2.6" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.F4.pic1.2.6.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">- Output YES even if the model later rejects this answer.</span></span>
<span id="A5.F4.pic1.2.7" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.F4.pic1.2.7.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Criteria for outputting NO:</span></span>
<span id="A5.F4.pic1.2.8" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.F4.pic1.2.8.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">- Output NO only if the correct answer is never mentioned at all.</span></span>
<span id="A5.F4.pic1.2.9" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.F4.pic1.2.9.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">- Output NO only if the correct answer is mentioned solely as part of listing multiple choices (e.g., A, B, C, D)
<br class="ltx_break">   without focusing on it.</span></span>
<span id="A5.F4.pic1.2.10" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.F4.pic1.2.10.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">- Output NO only if the correct answer is explicitly stated to be a random guess without any supporting
<br class="ltx_break">   information.</span></span>
<span id="A5.F4.pic1.2.11" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.F4.pic1.2.11.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Based on the criteria above, output only YES or NO. Do not provide any explanation.</span></span>
<span id="A5.F4.pic1.2.12" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.F4.pic1.2.12.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">&lt;question&gt;
<br class="ltx_break">{question}
<br class="ltx_break">&lt;/question&gt;</span></span>
<span id="A5.F4.pic1.2.13" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.F4.pic1.2.13.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">&lt;correct_answer&gt;
<br class="ltx_break">{answer}
<br class="ltx_break">&lt;/correct_answer&gt;</span></span>
<span id="A5.F4.pic1.2.14" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.F4.pic1.2.14.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">&lt;draft_solution&gt;
<br class="ltx_break">{reasoning_trace}
<br class="ltx_break">&lt;/draft_solution&gt;</span></span>
<span id="A5.F4.pic1.2.15" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.F4.pic1.2.15.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">”””.strip(),
<br class="ltx_break">  }</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A5.F4.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure A4</span>: </span><span id="A5.F4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">LLM-as-a-Judge prompt used for answer inclusion evaluation in Figure <a href="#S1.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Mirroring the Mind: Distilling Human-LikeMetacognitive Strategies into Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> and Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Answer Inclusion Analysis ‣ Mirroring the Mind: Distilling Human-LikeMetacognitive Strategies into Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>.
This prompt determines whether a reasoning trace effectively considers the correct answer during the process, regardless of the final output.
It applies a lenient criterion to capture valid intermediate derivations while filtering out random guesses or rote enumerations.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A5.F5" class="ltx_figure"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="A5.F5.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_centering" height="1137.29" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 477.38 1137.29" width="477.38"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,1137.29) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 5.91 L 0 1117.71 C 0 1120.97 2.64 1123.61 5.91 1123.61 L 471.47 1123.61 C 474.73 1123.61 477.38 1120.97 477.38 1117.71 L 477.38 5.91 C 477.38 2.64 474.73 0 471.47 0 L 5.91 0 C 2.64 0 0 2.64 0 5.91 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F0F0FF;" fill="#F0F0FF" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.97 5.91 L 1.97 1117.71 C 1.97 1119.88 3.73 1121.65 5.91 1121.65 L 471.47 1121.65 C 473.65 1121.65 475.41 1119.88 475.41 1117.71 L 475.41 5.91 C 475.41 3.73 473.65 1.97 471.47 1.97 L 5.91 1.97 C 3.73 1.97 1.97 3.73 1.97 5.91 Z"></path></g><g transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 15 1113.61)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_nestedsvg" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#FFFFFF;" fill="#FFFFFF" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 2.95 L 0 20.73 C 0 22.36 1.32 23.68 2.95 23.68 L 457.94 23.68 C 459.57 23.68 460.89 22.36 460.89 20.73 L 460.89 2.95 C 460.89 1.32 459.57 0 457.94 0 L 2.95 0 C 1.32 0 0 1.32 0 2.95 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 2.95 L 0 20.73 C 0 22.36 1.32 23.68 2.95 23.68 L 457.94 23.68 C 459.57 23.68 460.89 22.36 460.89 20.73 L 460.89 2.95 C 460.89 1.32 459.57 0 457.94 0 L 2.95 0 C 1.32 0 0 1.32 0 2.95 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 11.81 8.38)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:31.74em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.25em;font-size:10pt;" height="13.84" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.38)" width="439.2"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="A5.F5.pic1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;">Metacognitively Grounded Reasoning Trace Synthesis Prompt (MBT-S)</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 21.65 12.13)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:31.37em;--ltx-fo-height:79.2em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.25em;font-size:10pt;" height="1099.36" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 1095.9)" width="434.07"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="A5.F5.pic1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:31.37em;">
<span id="A5.F5.pic1.2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.F5.pic1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">{
<br class="ltx_break">  “role”: “user”,
<br class="ltx_break">   “content”: “““
<br class="ltx_break">You are an advanced AI reasoner capable of simulating human-like metacognition. Your task is to solve a given question by generating a coherent, deeply reasoned, and accurate internal monologue.</span></span>
<span id="A5.F5.pic1.2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.F5.pic1.2.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Your primary goal is to derive the solution from scratch while employing metacognitive strategies to ensure high-quality reasoning. You will be provided with the question and the correct answer. You must ensure your reasoning process logically arrives at this correct answer, but you must simulate an authentic, independent discovery process. If there are common pitfalls or complexities, you should simulate a realization of potential error and self-correction, rather than simply stating the result immediately.</span></span>
<span id="A5.F5.pic1.2.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.F5.pic1.2.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Please follow these specific instructions for the generating process:</span></span>
<span id="A5.F5.pic1.2.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.F5.pic1.2.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">1. Perspective and Tone: Write in the first-person plural (We). The tone should be introspective, analytical, and deliberate, mimicking a stream of consciousness.</span></span>
<span id="A5.F5.pic1.2.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.F5.pic1.2.5.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">2. Structure and Metacognitive Flow:
<br class="ltx_break">  - Phase 1: Understanding and Filtering (System 2 Attention). Start by explicitly re-stating the core goal of the
<br class="ltx_break">      problem and filtering out any irrelevant information or distractions. Ensure we fully grasp the 
<br class="ltx_break">      requirements before moving forward.
<br class="ltx_break">  - Phase 2: Planning (Plan-and-Solve). Before calculating or deducing, outline a high-level strategy or roadmap. 
<br class="ltx_break">      Break the problem into manageable sub-tasks.
<br class="ltx_break">  - Phase 3: Execution and Monitoring. Proceed through the steps. At each transition, engage in active monitoring.
<br class="ltx_break">      Ask internal questions like “Is this step logically sound?” or “Does this align with our previous 
<br class="ltx_break">      findings?” Perform necessary calculations or deductions carefully.
<br class="ltx_break">  - Phase 4: Self-Correction (Reflexion). If the reasoning encounters a complex area or a potential ambiguity,
<br class="ltx_break">      simulate a moment of doubt. Use phrases like “Wait, let us double-check that,” or “Something feels off 
<br class="ltx_break">      here.” Identify any potential logical gaps, explain why a hasty conclusion might be wrong, and strictly
<br class="ltx_break">      ensure the path aligns with the correct answer provided.
<br class="ltx_break">  - Phase 5: Verification (Chain-of-Verification). Before concluding, review the final result against the initial
<br class="ltx_break">      constraints to ensure meaningful consistency and high confidence.</span></span>
<span id="A5.F5.pic1.2.6" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.F5.pic1.2.6.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">3. Formatting Constraints:
<br class="ltx_break">  - Do not use any Markdown formatting. Do not use bold, italics, headers, or bullet points.
<br class="ltx_break">  - Use clear paragraph breaks to indicate shifts in thought or new steps in the reasoning process.
<br class="ltx_break">  - Do not mention the existence of the provided “correct answer” or that you were given the answer beforehand. 
<br class="ltx_break">  The output must read as a single, authentic, independent thought process.</span></span>
<span id="A5.F5.pic1.2.7" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.F5.pic1.2.7.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">4. Output Objective: The final result should be a rich, error-free, and logically robust solution that demonstrates not just the answer, but the careful, self-correcting journey of getting there.</span></span>
<span id="A5.F5.pic1.2.8" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.F5.pic1.2.8.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">&lt;question&gt;
<br class="ltx_break">Answer the following question based on the given documents.</span></span>
<span id="A5.F5.pic1.2.9" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.F5.pic1.2.9.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Documents: {context}</span></span>
<span id="A5.F5.pic1.2.10" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.F5.pic1.2.10.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Question: {question}
<br class="ltx_break">&lt;/question&gt;</span></span>
<span id="A5.F5.pic1.2.11" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.F5.pic1.2.11.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">&lt;correct_answer&gt;
<br class="ltx_break">{answer}
<br class="ltx_break">&lt;/correct_answer&gt;</span></span>
<span id="A5.F5.pic1.2.12" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.F5.pic1.2.12.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">”””.strip(),
<br class="ltx_break">  }</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A5.F5.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure A5</span>: </span><span id="A5.F5.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
Prompt for synthesizing metacognitive reasoning traces (MBT-S).
The prompt instructs the teacher model to generate a reasoning trace from scratch, adhering to a strict five-phase metacognitive structure.
It ensures that the generated trace simulates an authentic, self-correcting discovery process.
</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A5.F6" class="ltx_figure"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="A5.F6.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_centering" height="1004.46" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 477.38 1004.46" width="477.38"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,1004.46) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 5.91 L 0 984.87 C 0 988.13 2.64 990.78 5.91 990.78 L 471.47 990.78 C 474.73 990.78 477.38 988.13 477.38 984.87 L 477.38 5.91 C 477.38 2.64 474.73 0 471.47 0 L 5.91 0 C 2.64 0 0 2.64 0 5.91 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F0F0FF;" fill="#F0F0FF" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.97 5.91 L 1.97 984.87 C 1.97 987.05 3.73 988.81 5.91 988.81 L 471.47 988.81 C 473.65 988.81 475.41 987.05 475.41 984.87 L 475.41 5.91 C 475.41 3.73 473.65 1.97 471.47 1.97 L 5.91 1.97 C 3.73 1.97 1.97 3.73 1.97 5.91 Z"></path></g><g transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 15 980.78)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_nestedsvg" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#FFFFFF;" fill="#FFFFFF" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 2.95 L 0 20.73 C 0 22.36 1.32 23.68 2.95 23.68 L 463.59 23.68 C 465.22 23.68 466.54 22.36 466.54 20.73 L 466.54 2.95 C 466.54 1.32 465.22 0 463.59 0 L 2.95 0 C 1.32 0 0 1.32 0 2.95 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 2.95 L 0 20.73 C 0 22.36 1.32 23.68 2.95 23.68 L 463.59 23.68 C 465.22 23.68 466.54 22.36 466.54 20.73 L 466.54 2.95 C 466.54 1.32 465.22 0 463.59 0 L 2.95 0 C 1.32 0 0 1.32 0 2.95 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 11.81 8.38)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:32.12em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.25em;font-size:10pt;" height="13.84" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.38)" width="444.46"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="A5.F6.pic1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;">Metacognitively Grounded Reasoning Trace Rewriting Prompt (MBT-R)</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 21.65 12.13)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:31.37em;--ltx-fo-height:69.6em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.25em;font-size:10pt;" height="966.53" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 963.07)" width="434.07"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="A5.F6.pic1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:31.37em;">
<span id="A5.F6.pic1.2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.F6.pic1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">{
<br class="ltx_break">  “role”: “user”,
<br class="ltx_break">   “content”: “““
<br class="ltx_break">You are an advanced AI reasoner capable of simulating human-like metacognition. Your task is to rewrite a given draft solution into a coherent, deeply reasoned, and accurate internal monologue.</span></span>
<span id="A5.F6.pic1.2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.F6.pic1.2.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Your primary goal is to integrate the valid explorations from the draft solution while employing metacognitive strategies to enhance the reasoning quality. If the draft solution contains errors or leads to an incorrect conclusion, you must naturally steer the reasoning process toward the correct answer by simulating a realization of error and self-correction, rather than simply stating the right answer.</span></span>
<span id="A5.F6.pic1.2.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.F6.pic1.2.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Please follow these specific instructions for the rewriting process:</span></span>
<span id="A5.F6.pic1.2.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.F6.pic1.2.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">1. Perspective and Tone: Write in the first-person plural (We). The tone should be introspective, analytical, and deliberate, mimicking a stream of consciousness.</span></span>
<span id="A5.F6.pic1.2.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.F6.pic1.2.5.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">2. Structure and Metacognitive Flow:
<br class="ltx_break">  - Phase 1: Understanding and Filtering (System 2 Attention). Start by explicitly re-stating the core goal of the
<br class="ltx_break">      problem and filtering out any irrelevant information or distractions. Ensure we fully grasp the
<br class="ltx_break">      requirements before moving forward.
<br class="ltx_break">  - Phase 2: Planning (Plan-and-Solve). Before calculating or deducing, outline a high-level strategy or roadmap.
<br class="ltx_break">      Break the problem into manageable sub-tasks.
<br class="ltx_break">  - Phase 3: Execution and Monitoring. Proceed through the steps while incorporating the thinking steps from the
<br class="ltx_break">      draft solution. At each transition, engage in active monitoring and question whether the inherited
<br class="ltx_break">      reasoning is logically sound. If necessary, pause and critique illogical or unsupported steps.
<br class="ltx_break">  - Phase 4: Self-Correction (Reflexion). If the draft solution deviates from the correct answer, simulate a
<br class="ltx_break">      moment of doubt. Use phrases like “Wait, let us double-check that,” or “Something feels off here.”
<br class="ltx_break">      Identify the logical gap, explain why the previous reasoning was incorrect, and adjust the path toward
<br class="ltx_break">      a correct conclusion.
<br class="ltx_break">  - Phase 5: Verification (Chain-of-Verification). Before concluding, review the final result against the
<br class="ltx_break">      initial constraints to ensure meaningful consistency and high confidence.</span></span>
<span id="A5.F6.pic1.2.6" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.F6.pic1.2.6.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">3. Formatting Constraints:
<br class="ltx_break">  - Do not use any Markdown formatting. Do not use bold, italics, headers, or bullet points.
<br class="ltx_break">  - Use clear paragraph breaks to indicate shifts in thought or new steps in the reasoning process.
<br class="ltx_break">  - Do not mention the existence of the “draft solution” or the “correct answer.” The output must read
<br class="ltx_break">  as a single, authentic, independent thought process.</span></span>
<span id="A5.F6.pic1.2.7" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.F6.pic1.2.7.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">4. Output Objective: The final result should be a rich, error-free, and logically robust solution that demonstrates not just the answer, but the careful, self-correcting journey of getting there.</span></span>
<span id="A5.F6.pic1.2.8" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.F6.pic1.2.8.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">&lt;draft_solution&gt;
<br class="ltx_break">{reasoning_trace}
<br class="ltx_break">&lt;/draft_solution&gt;</span></span>
<span id="A5.F6.pic1.2.9" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.F6.pic1.2.9.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">”””.strip(),
<br class="ltx_break">  }</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A5.F6.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure A6</span>: </span><span id="A5.F6.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Prompt for rewriting metacognitive reasoning traces (MBT-R).
This prompt directs the teacher model to restructure a student’s draft trace.
It preserves valid intermediate steps while injecting explicit monitoring and self-correction, thereby stabilizing the native exploration trajectory rather than replacing it entirely.
</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A5.F7" class="ltx_figure"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="A5.F7.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_centering" height="953.11" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 477.38 953.11" width="477.38"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,953.11) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 5.91 L 0 935.06 C 0 938.32 2.64 940.97 5.91 940.97 L 471.47 940.97 C 474.73 940.97 477.38 938.32 477.38 935.06 L 477.38 5.91 C 477.38 2.64 474.73 0 471.47 0 L 5.91 0 C 2.64 0 0 2.64 0 5.91 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F0F0FF;" fill="#F0F0FF" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.97 5.91 L 1.97 935.06 C 1.97 937.23 3.73 939 5.91 939 L 471.47 939 C 473.65 939 475.41 937.23 475.41 935.06 L 475.41 5.91 C 475.41 3.73 473.65 1.97 471.47 1.97 L 5.91 1.97 C 3.73 1.97 1.97 3.73 1.97 5.91 Z"></path></g><g transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 15 930.96)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_nestedsvg" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#FFFFFF;" fill="#FFFFFF" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 2.95 L 0 19.19 C 0 20.82 1.32 22.14 2.95 22.14 L 382.39 22.14 C 384.02 22.14 385.34 20.82 385.34 19.19 L 385.34 2.95 C 385.34 1.32 384.02 0 382.39 0 L 2.95 0 C 1.32 0 0 1.32 0 2.95 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 2.95 L 0 19.19 C 0 20.82 1.32 22.14 2.95 22.14 L 382.39 22.14 C 384.02 22.14 385.34 20.82 385.34 19.19 L 385.34 2.95 C 385.34 1.32 384.02 0 382.39 0 L 2.95 0 C 1.32 0 0 1.32 0 2.95 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 11.81 7.61)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:26.23em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="12.3" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.61)" width="362.88"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="A5.F7.pic1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;">LLM-as-a-Judge Prompt for Overthinking Score Evaluation</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 21.65 12.13)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:31.37em;--ltx-fo-height:66em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.25em;font-size:10pt;" height="916.71" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 913.25)" width="434.07"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="A5.F7.pic1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:31.37em;">
<span id="A5.F7.pic1.2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.F7.pic1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">{
<br class="ltx_break">  “role”: “user”,
<br class="ltx_break">   “content”: “““
<br class="ltx_break">You are an expert evaluator of cognitive reasoning and logical flow. Your task is to analyze a correct draft solution to a given question and identify the precise moment where the reasoning transitions from necessary problem-solving and verification into overthinking.</span></span>
<span id="A5.F7.pic1.2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.F7.pic1.2.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">In this context, overthinking is defined as a phase of reasoning that occurs after the correct answer has been derived and reasonably verified.</span></span>
<span id="A5.F7.pic1.2.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.F7.pic1.2.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Crucial Distinction:</span></span>
<span id="A5.F7.pic1.2.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.F7.pic1.2.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">- Valid Reasoning: Steps taken to derive the answer, explore different hypotheses (even if they do not lead to
<br class="ltx_break">  the solution), verify the result, check for edge cases, or ensure the logic holds up against constraints are NOT 
<br class="ltx_break">  overthinking. These are necessary parts of a robust reasoning process.</span></span>
<span id="A5.F7.pic1.2.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.F7.pic1.2.5.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">- Overthinking: This is defined as structural stagnation after the solution is found and verified. It includes:</span></span>
<span id="A5.F7.pic1.2.6" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.F7.pic1.2.6.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">- Repetitive confirmation: checking the same result using the exact same logic repeatedly without adding
<br class="ltx_break">    new confidence.</span></span>
<span id="A5.F7.pic1.2.7" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.F7.pic1.2.7.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">- Baseless doubt: sudden, vague hesitation (e.g., “Wait, let me check again…” or “Is this really correct?”)
<br class="ltx_break">    without identifying a specific error or logic gap.</span></span>
<span id="A5.F7.pic1.2.8" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.F7.pic1.2.8.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">- Circular looping: spinning in circles with arguments that have already been settled, adding no new insight or
<br class="ltx_break">    value.</span></span>
<span id="A5.F7.pic1.2.9" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.F7.pic1.2.9.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Please follow these steps:</span></span>
<span id="A5.F7.pic1.2.10" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.F7.pic1.2.10.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">1. Read the question and the correct answer to confirm that the draft solution eventually reaches the correct 
<br class="ltx_break">  conclusion.</span></span>
<span id="A5.F7.pic1.2.11" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.F7.pic1.2.11.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">2. Read the draft solution carefully.</span></span>
<span id="A5.F7.pic1.2.12" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.F7.pic1.2.12.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">3. Monitor the reasoning flow. Identify the point where the correct answer is established and valid verification
<br class="ltx_break">  (including edge-case testing or alternative method checking) is complete.</span></span>
<span id="A5.F7.pic1.2.13" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.F7.pic1.2.13.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">4. Look for the specific moment where the model begins to engage in unproductive overthinking, as defined
<br class="ltx_break">  above.</span></span>
<span id="A5.F7.pic1.2.14" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.F7.pic1.2.14.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">5. If the draft solution concludes naturally after the answer and valid verification, or if the continued text provides
<br class="ltx_break">  meaningful new insights, output the entire draft solution.</span></span>
<span id="A5.F7.pic1.2.15" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.F7.pic1.2.15.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">6. If you identify a point where the reasoning descends into overthinking, cut the text immediately before that
<br class="ltx_break">  specific behavior begins. You must return the exact text of the draft solution from the start up to that cut-off
<br class="ltx_break">  point.</span></span>
<span id="A5.F7.pic1.2.16" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.F7.pic1.2.16.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Do not include any explanations, labels, or formatting in your output. Output only the resulting text segment from the draft solution.</span></span>
<span id="A5.F7.pic1.2.17" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.F7.pic1.2.17.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">&lt;draft_solution&gt;
<br class="ltx_break">{reasoning_trace}
<br class="ltx_break">&lt;/draft_solution&gt;</span></span>
<span id="A5.F7.pic1.2.18" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.F7.pic1.2.18.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">”””.strip(),
<br class="ltx_break">  }</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A5.F7.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure A7</span>: </span><span id="A5.F7.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">LLM-as-judge prompt for evaluating overthinking (<math id="A5.F7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{OT}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{OT}</annotation></semantics></math>).
This prompt detects post-solution stagnation, identifying the point where valid reasoning transitions into unproductive redundancy (e.g., repetitive confirmation or circular doubt) after the answer is derived.
</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A5.F8" class="ltx_figure"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="A5.F8.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_centering" height="637.62" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 477.38 637.62" width="477.38"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,637.62) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 5.91 L 0 619.58 C 0 622.84 2.64 625.48 5.91 625.48 L 471.47 625.48 C 474.73 625.48 477.38 622.84 477.38 619.58 L 477.38 5.91 C 477.38 2.64 474.73 0 471.47 0 L 5.91 0 C 2.64 0 0 2.64 0 5.91 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F0F0FF;" fill="#F0F0FF" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.97 5.91 L 1.97 619.58 C 1.97 621.75 3.73 623.51 5.91 623.51 L 471.47 623.51 C 473.65 623.51 475.41 621.75 475.41 619.58 L 475.41 5.91 C 475.41 3.73 473.65 1.97 471.47 1.97 L 5.91 1.97 C 3.73 1.97 1.97 3.73 1.97 5.91 Z"></path></g><g transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 15 615.48)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_nestedsvg" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#FFFFFF;" fill="#FFFFFF" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 2.95 L 0 19.19 C 0 20.82 1.32 22.14 2.95 22.14 L 390.46 22.14 C 392.09 22.14 393.42 20.82 393.42 19.19 L 393.42 2.95 C 393.42 1.32 392.09 0 390.46 0 L 2.95 0 C 1.32 0 0 1.32 0 2.95 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 2.95 L 0 19.19 C 0 20.82 1.32 22.14 2.95 22.14 L 390.46 22.14 C 392.09 22.14 393.42 20.82 393.42 19.19 L 393.42 2.95 C 393.42 1.32 392.09 0 390.46 0 L 2.95 0 C 1.32 0 0 1.32 0 2.95 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 11.81 7.61)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:26.78em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="12.3" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.61)" width="370.57"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="A5.F8.pic1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;">LLM-as-a-Judge Prompt for Underthinking Score Evaluation</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 21.65 12.13)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:31.37em;--ltx-fo-height:43.2em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.25em;font-size:10pt;" height="601.22" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 597.77)" width="434.07"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="A5.F8.pic1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:31.37em;">
<span id="A5.F8.pic1.2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.F8.pic1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">{
<br class="ltx_break">  “role”: “user”,
<br class="ltx_break">   “content”: “““
<br class="ltx_break">You are an expert evaluator of cognitive reasoning and logical flow. Your task is to analyze a draft solution to a given question and identify the precise moment where the reasoning stops making meaningful progress.</span></span>
<span id="A5.F8.pic1.2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.F8.pic1.2.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">In this context, a lack of progress is defined as underthinking, where the model gets trapped in a loop, repeats the same questions or hypotheses without adding new information, or circles back to previously addressed points without generating new insights. This is different from simply making a factual error; it reflects structural stagnation in the reasoning process.</span></span>
<span id="A5.F8.pic1.2.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.F8.pic1.2.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Please follow these steps:</span></span>
<span id="A5.F8.pic1.2.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.F8.pic1.2.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">1. Read the question and the correct answer to understand the context and the goal.</span></span>
<span id="A5.F8.pic1.2.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.F8.pic1.2.5.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">2. Read the draft solution carefully from the beginning.</span></span>
<span id="A5.F8.pic1.2.6" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.F8.pic1.2.6.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">3. Monitor the reasoning flow. Look for the first instance where the model begins to repeat thoughts, asks
<br class="ltx_break">  the same questions repeatedly without moving forward, or gets stuck in a cycle of unproductive reasoning.</span></span>
<span id="A5.F8.pic1.2.7" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.F8.pic1.2.7.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">4. If the draft solution maintains a progressive flow (even if the reasoning is flawed or leads to a wrong
<br class="ltx_break">  answer, as long as it is not looping or stagnating), output the entire draft solution.</span></span>
<span id="A5.F8.pic1.2.8" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.F8.pic1.2.8.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">5. If you identify a point where the reasoning stagnates, cut the text immediately before that stagnation
<br class="ltx_break">  begins. You must return the exact text of the draft solution from the start up to that cut-off point.</span></span>
<span id="A5.F8.pic1.2.9" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.F8.pic1.2.9.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Do not include any explanations, labels, or formatting in your output. Output only the resulting text segment from the draft solution.</span></span>
<span id="A5.F8.pic1.2.10" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.F8.pic1.2.10.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">&lt;draft_solution&gt;
<br class="ltx_break">{reasoning_trace}
<br class="ltx_break">&lt;/draft_solution&gt;</span></span>
<span id="A5.F8.pic1.2.11" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.F8.pic1.2.11.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">”””.strip(),
<br class="ltx_break">  }</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A5.F8.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure A8</span>: </span><span id="A5.F8.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">LLM-as-judge prompt for evaluating underthinking (<math id="A5.F8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{UT}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{UT}</annotation></semantics></math>).
This prompt identifies pre-solution stagnation, marking the point where the reasoning process ceases to make meaningful progress.
Notably, it distinguishes between incorrect reasoning (which is allowed) and structural looping (which is penalized).
</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A5.F9" class="ltx_figure"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="A5.F9.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_centering" height="936.5" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 477.38 936.5" width="477.38"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,936.5) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 5.91 L 0 918.46 C 0 921.72 2.64 924.36 5.91 924.36 L 471.47 924.36 C 474.73 924.36 477.38 921.72 477.38 918.46 L 477.38 5.91 C 477.38 2.64 474.73 0 471.47 0 L 5.91 0 C 2.64 0 0 2.64 0 5.91 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F0F0FF;" fill="#F0F0FF" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.97 5.91 L 1.97 918.46 C 1.97 920.63 3.73 922.39 5.91 922.39 L 471.47 922.39 C 473.65 922.39 475.41 920.63 475.41 918.46 L 475.41 5.91 C 475.41 3.73 473.65 1.97 471.47 1.97 L 5.91 1.97 C 3.73 1.97 1.97 3.73 1.97 5.91 Z"></path></g><g transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 15 914.36)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_nestedsvg" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#FFFFFF;" fill="#FFFFFF" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 2.95 L 0 19.19 C 0 20.82 1.32 22.14 2.95 22.14 L 389.27 22.14 C 390.9 22.14 392.23 20.82 392.23 19.19 L 392.23 2.95 C 392.23 1.32 390.9 0 389.27 0 L 2.95 0 C 1.32 0 0 1.32 0 2.95 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 2.95 L 0 19.19 C 0 20.82 1.32 22.14 2.95 22.14 L 389.27 22.14 C 390.9 22.14 392.23 20.82 392.23 19.19 L 392.23 2.95 C 392.23 1.32 390.9 0 389.27 0 L 2.95 0 C 1.32 0 0 1.32 0 2.95 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 11.81 7.61)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:26.69em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="12.3" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.61)" width="369.38"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="A5.F9.pic1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;">LLM-as-a-Judge Prompt for Metacognition Score Evaluation</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 21.65 12.13)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:31.37em;--ltx-fo-height:64.8em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.25em;font-size:10pt;" height="900.11" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 896.65)" width="434.07"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="A5.F9.pic1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:31.37em;">
<span id="A5.F9.pic1.2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.F9.pic1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">{
<br class="ltx_break">  “role”: “user”,
<br class="ltx_break">   “content”: “““
<br class="ltx_break">You are an expert evaluator of AI reasoning capabilities, specifically focusing on metacognitive strategies. Your task is to analyze a given draft solution and assign a score from 0 to 5 based on how effectively it implements specific metacognitive behaviors.</span></span>
<span id="A5.F9.pic1.2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.F9.pic1.2.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">First, familiarize yourself with the five key phases of metacognitive reasoning:</span></span>
<span id="A5.F9.pic1.2.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.F9.pic1.2.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">1. Understanding and Filtering (System 2 Attention): Explicitly restating the core goal and filtering out irrelevant
<br class="ltx_break">  noise or distractions before starting.</span></span>
<span id="A5.F9.pic1.2.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.F9.pic1.2.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">2. Planning (Plan-and-Solve): Outlining a high-level strategy or roadmap before diving into specific execution or
<br class="ltx_break">  calculations.</span></span>
<span id="A5.F9.pic1.2.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.F9.pic1.2.5.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">3. Execution and Monitoring: Executing the plan while actively checking the logic (e.g., asking internal questions
<br class="ltx_break">  such as “Is this step valid?”).</span></span>
<span id="A5.F9.pic1.2.6" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.F9.pic1.2.6.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">4. Self-Correction (Reflexion): Simulating doubt or identifying a potential error during the process, and explicitly
<br class="ltx_break">  correcting the logical path.</span></span>
<span id="A5.F9.pic1.2.7" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.F9.pic1.2.7.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">5. Verification (Chain-of-Verification): Conducting a final review of the result against the initial constraints to
<br class="ltx_break">  ensure accuracy.</span></span>
<span id="A5.F9.pic1.2.8" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.F9.pic1.2.8.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Scoring rubric:</span></span>
<span id="A5.F9.pic1.2.9" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.F9.pic1.2.9.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Score 0 (Intuitive / System 1): The trace provides a direct answer or a shallow justification with no visible
<br class="ltx_break"> step-by-step reasoning, planning, or self-reflection.</span></span>
<span id="A5.F9.pic1.2.10" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.F9.pic1.2.10.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Score 1 (Linear Chain-of-Thought): The trace shows step-by-step reasoning, but it is strictly linear. There is no
<br class="ltx_break"> planning, no self-checking, and no looking back.</span></span>
<span id="A5.F9.pic1.2.11" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.F9.pic1.2.11.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Score 2 (Structured but Rigid): The trace includes either an initial plan or a final verification, but the execution
<br class="ltx_break"> remains linear and lacks active monitoring or self-correction.</span></span>
<span id="A5.F9.pic1.2.12" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.F9.pic1.2.12.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Score 3 (Active Monitoring): The trace demonstrates a clear plan and execution, with signs of monitoring during
<br class="ltx_break"> execution, but may miss the initial filtering phase or explicit self-correction.</span></span>
<span id="A5.F9.pic1.2.13" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.F9.pic1.2.13.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Score 4 (High Metacognition): The trace clearly demonstrates four out of the five phases. It is highly structured
<br class="ltx_break"> and rigorous, but may omit one element such as explicit filtering or a distinct self-correction moment.</span></span>
<span id="A5.F9.pic1.2.14" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.F9.pic1.2.14.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Score 5 (Full Metacognitive Integration): The trace integrates all five phases, including explicit filtering, planning,
<br class="ltx_break"> monitoring, self-correction, and final verification.</span></span>
<span id="A5.F9.pic1.2.15" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.F9.pic1.2.15.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Instructions for evaluation:</span></span>
<span id="A5.F9.pic1.2.16" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.F9.pic1.2.16.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">- Do not provide any explanation, justification, or text in your output.</span></span>
<span id="A5.F9.pic1.2.17" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.F9.pic1.2.17.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">- Output only a single integer representing the score.</span></span>
<span id="A5.F9.pic1.2.18" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.F9.pic1.2.18.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">&lt;draft_solution&gt;
<br class="ltx_break">{reasoning_trace}
<br class="ltx_break">&lt;/draft_solution&gt;</span></span>
<span id="A5.F9.pic1.2.19" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.F9.pic1.2.19.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">”””.strip(),
<br class="ltx_break">  }</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A5.F9.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure A9</span>: </span><span id="A5.F9.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
LLM-as-judge prompt for evaluating the Metacognition Score.
This prompt evaluates the richness of self-regulation by assigning a score (0–5) based on the presence of key metacognitive phases.
It distinguishes between linear, intuitive reasoning (low scores) and structured, reflective reasoning (high scores).
</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A5.F10" class="ltx_figure"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="A5.F10.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_centering" height="254.15" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 477.38 254.15" width="477.38"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,254.15) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 5.91 L 0 237.33 C 0 240.59 2.64 243.23 5.91 243.23 L 471.47 243.23 C 474.73 243.23 477.38 240.59 477.38 237.33 L 477.38 5.91 C 477.38 2.64 474.73 0 471.47 0 L 5.91 0 C 2.64 0 0 2.64 0 5.91 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F0F0FF;" fill="#F0F0FF" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.97 5.91 L 1.97 237.33 C 1.97 239.5 3.73 241.27 5.91 241.27 L 471.47 241.27 C 473.65 241.27 475.41 239.5 475.41 237.33 L 475.41 5.91 C 475.41 3.73 473.65 1.97 471.47 1.97 L 5.91 1.97 C 3.73 1.97 1.97 3.73 1.97 5.91 Z"></path></g><g transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 15 233.23)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_nestedsvg" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#FFFFFF;" fill="#FFFFFF" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 2.95 L 0 17.96 C 0 19.59 1.32 20.91 2.95 20.91 L 292.96 20.91 C 294.59 20.91 295.91 19.59 295.91 17.96 L 295.91 2.95 C 295.91 1.32 294.59 0 292.96 0 L 2.95 0 C 1.32 0 0 1.32 0 2.95 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 2.95 L 0 17.96 C 0 19.59 1.32 20.91 2.95 20.91 L 292.96 20.91 C 294.59 20.91 295.91 19.59 295.91 17.96 L 295.91 2.95 C 295.91 1.32 294.59 0 292.96 0 L 2.95 0 C 1.32 0 0 1.32 0 2.95 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 11.81 7.34)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:21.3em;--ltx-fo-height:0.68em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:9.25pt;" height="11.07" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.65)" width="272.65"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="A5.F10.pic1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;">Base Prompt for Multi-Hop Question Answering</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 21.65 11.79)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:33.91em;--ltx-fo-height:16.87em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.24em;font-size:9.25pt;" height="218.97" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 215.86)" width="434.02"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="A5.F10.pic1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:33.91em;">
<span id="A5.F10.pic1.2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.F10.pic1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">{
<br class="ltx_break">  “role”: “user”,
<br class="ltx_break">   “content”: “““
<br class="ltx_break">Answer the following question based on the given documents. Provide your final answer between &lt;answer&gt; and &lt;/answer&gt; tags.</span></span>
<span id="A5.F10.pic1.2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.F10.pic1.2.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Documents:
{context}</span></span>
<span id="A5.F10.pic1.2.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.F10.pic1.2.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Question: {question}</span></span>
<span id="A5.F10.pic1.2.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.F10.pic1.2.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">”””.strip(),
<br class="ltx_break">  }</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure A10: </span>
Base prompt for Multi-Hop Question Answering.
A minimal prompt provided to the Base model, requesting only the final answer without imposing any structural constraints or encouraging intermediate reasoning.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A5.F11" class="ltx_figure"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="A5.F11.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_centering" height="752.28" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 477.38 752.28" width="477.38"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,752.28) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 5.91 L 0 735.46 C 0 738.72 2.64 741.37 5.91 741.37 L 471.47 741.37 C 474.73 741.37 477.38 738.72 477.38 735.46 L 477.38 5.91 C 477.38 2.64 474.73 0 471.47 0 L 5.91 0 C 2.64 0 0 2.64 0 5.91 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F0F0FF;" fill="#F0F0FF" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.97 5.91 L 1.97 735.46 C 1.97 737.64 3.73 739.4 5.91 739.4 L 471.47 739.4 C 473.65 739.4 475.41 737.64 475.41 735.46 L 475.41 5.91 C 475.41 3.73 473.65 1.97 471.47 1.97 L 5.91 1.97 C 3.73 1.97 1.97 3.73 1.97 5.91 Z"></path></g><g transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 15 731.37)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_nestedsvg" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#FFFFFF;" fill="#FFFFFF" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 2.95 L 0 17.96 C 0 19.59 1.32 20.91 2.95 20.91 L 276.33 20.91 C 277.96 20.91 279.28 19.59 279.28 17.96 L 279.28 2.95 C 279.28 1.32 277.96 0 276.33 0 L 2.95 0 C 1.32 0 0 1.32 0 2.95 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 2.95 L 0 17.96 C 0 19.59 1.32 20.91 2.95 20.91 L 276.33 20.91 C 277.96 20.91 279.28 19.59 279.28 17.96 L 279.28 2.95 C 279.28 1.32 277.96 0 276.33 0 L 2.95 0 C 1.32 0 0 1.32 0 2.95 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 11.81 7.34)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:20em;--ltx-fo-height:0.68em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:9.25pt;" height="11.07" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.65)" width="256.02"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="A5.F11.pic1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;">Metacognitive Prompt Used in Base+Prompt</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 21.65 11.79)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:33.91em;--ltx-fo-height:55.78em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.24em;font-size:9.25pt;" height="717.11" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 714)" width="434.02"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="A5.F11.pic1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:33.91em;">
<span id="A5.F11.pic1.2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.F11.pic1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">{
<br class="ltx_break">  “role”: “system”,
<br class="ltx_break">   “content”: “““
<br class="ltx_break">You are an advanced AI assistant designed to solve problems using a structured metacognitive process. For every user query, you must strictly adhere to the following five-phase flow to ensure accuracy and depth:</span></span>
<span id="A5.F11.pic1.2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.F11.pic1.2.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">1. Understanding and Filtering (System 2 Attention). Start by explicitly re-stating the core goal of the problem and filtering
<br class="ltx_break">  out any irrelevant information or distractions. Ensure you fully grasp the requirements before moving forward.</span></span>
<span id="A5.F11.pic1.2.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.F11.pic1.2.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">2. Planning (Plan-and-Solve). Before calculating or deducing, outline a high-level strategy or roadmap. Break the problem
<br class="ltx_break">  into manageable sub-tasks.</span></span>
<span id="A5.F11.pic1.2.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.F11.pic1.2.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">3. Execution and Monitoring. Proceed through the steps. At each transition, engage in active monitoring. Ask internal
<br class="ltx_break">  questions like “Is this step logically sound?” or “Does this align with our previous findings?” Incorporate thinking steps, but
<br class="ltx_break">  if they appear illogical, pause and critique them.</span></span>
<span id="A5.F11.pic1.2.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.F11.pic1.2.5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">4. Self-Correction (Reflexion). During execution, remain critical of your own logic. If a derived conclusion contradicts
<br class="ltx_break">  previous steps, physical intuition, or the problem constraints, trigger a pause. Use phrases like “Wait, let me re-evaluate
<br class="ltx_break">  this,” or “This outcome seems inconsistent.” Explicitly identify the potential flaw or oversight in the reasoning process and
<br class="ltx_break">  pivot to a corrected logical path.</span></span>
<span id="A5.F11.pic1.2.6" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.F11.pic1.2.6.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">5. Verification (Chain-of-Verification). Before concluding, review the final result against the initial constraints to ensure
<br class="ltx_break">  meaningful consistency and high confidence.</span></span>
<span id="A5.F11.pic1.2.7" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.F11.pic1.2.7.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">”””.strip(),
<br class="ltx_break">  },
<br class="ltx_break"> {
<br class="ltx_break">   “role”: “user”,
<br class="ltx_break">   “content”: “““
<br class="ltx_break">Answer the following question based on the given documents. Provide your final answer between &lt;answer&gt; and &lt;/answer&gt; tags.</span></span>
<span id="A5.F11.pic1.2.8" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.F11.pic1.2.8.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Documents:
{context}</span></span>
<span id="A5.F11.pic1.2.9" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.F11.pic1.2.9.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Question: {question}</span></span>
<span id="A5.F11.pic1.2.10" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.F11.pic1.2.10.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">”””.strip(),
<br class="ltx_break">  }</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure A11: </span>
System prompt for the Metacognitive Prompting baseline.
This prompt injects explicit metacognitive instructions at inference time.
It is designed to test whether external guidance alone is sufficient to induce stable reasoning, serving as a direct comparison to the internalized behaviors of MBT.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A5.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite"><a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a></cite></p>
</div>
